# The interface between Porelo and anything with a user in front of it.
#
# This file is the contract, not a description of one: the daemon serves it verbatim through
# org.varlink.service.GetInterfaceDescription, and a test fails the build when a method is
# registered without appearing here or appears here without being registered. A client may
# therefore read it off a running daemon and trust that it matches.
#
# Everything is reached over one unix socket, owner-only, in Porelo's runtime directory
# ($XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/porelo/porelod.sock). There is no port and no authentication of its own: the
# filesystem decides who may connect, and a remote client forwards the socket over SSH rather
# than reaching a listener.
#
# Framing is varlink's own: one JSON object per call, NUL-separated, no length prefix. A call
# carrying "more": true asks for a stream, and every reply but the last carries
# "continues": true.
#
# A field declared ?type is genuinely absent, not empty. Everything else is always present -
# a string that has no value arrives as "", because an interface has to render something.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- compatibility
#
# NOT IN FORCE YET. Everything below describes what this interface promises once there is a
# release. There is none: the version is a snapshot, there is no release tag, and the only
# clients are the daemon and the frontend in this same organisation, changed together by the
# people writing them. Until that stops being true, a method here may be renamed, removed or
# given a different meaning in place, and the frontend is adapted in the same breath - rather
# than growing a Tasks2 to carry a mistake nobody ever depended on.
#
# What this exemption does NOT license: silently changing a method while a client still reads
# the old meaning. The rule it replaces is "keep the old shape"; the rule it does not replace is
# "both sides agree, deliberately, and the change is written down".
#
# It ends at the first release, and the decision to release is the decision to freeze this. From
# then on the interface only grows, exactly as described below, and a shape that turns out wrong
# costs a Tasks2 instead of an edit.
#
# A fleet is not upgraded at once, so a frontend has to work against a daemon older than
# itself. Three things make that possible, and a client should use all three.
#
# 1. The trailing 1 in the interface name is the promise. Within it this interface only ever
#    grows: new methods, new ?parameters, new reply fields. Nothing that is here will be
#    removed, renamed, given a different type, or made to mean something else. A change that
#    cannot be made that way becomes org.fuin.porelo.Tasks2, and a daemon serves both for at
#    least one release - so a client reads org.varlink.service.GetInfo, takes the highest
#    interface name it understands from the interfaces list, and uses that. Version numbers
#    are not compared to decide this.
#
# 2. Per feature, not per connection. Calling a method an older daemon does not have answers
#    org.varlink.service.MethodNotFound. That is a normal answer, not a fault: an interface
#    disables that one feature and keeps everything else. The same goes for a ?parameter an
#    older daemon ignores - it will behave as though it were not given, which is what its
#    default says it does.
#
# 3. Unknown values must not be fatal. New reply fields will appear in replies a client did
#    not expect, and new values will appear in enumerations it does not know - Outcome in
#    particular will gain entries. A client MUST ignore fields it does not recognize and MUST
#    render an unrecognized enumeration value rather than failing on it. This is part of the
#    contract: adding a value is not treated as a breaking change, so a client that cannot
#    survive one will break on a routine release.
#
# org.varlink.service.GetInfo also reports the daemon's own version, which is a build
# version and moves independently of the number in the interface name. Show it, log it, and
# put it in a bug report - but do not gate features on it. What a daemon can do is answered
# by 1 and 2 above, which stay true no matter how the product is numbered.
#
# org.varlink.service.GetInterfaceDescription returns this file, from the running daemon.
# A client that wants certainty rather than assumption can read the contract off the thing
# it is talking to.

interface org.fuin.porelo.Tasks1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- types

# Who granted an authorization, and when. Not a secret: the audit fact that a task acts as this person.
type Authorization (
  # The account that ran the grant.
  grantedBy: string,
  # When, ISO 8601.
  grantedAt: string
)

# A project's conversation, on a transport that keeps one.
type ProjectMessages (
  # The transport it is on, the scheme a peer's address names.
  transport: string,
  # What the transport calls it, "" before it was set up on this machine.
  conversation: string,
  # What the project's messages reach through it, as the transport says.
  reaches: []string,
  # Whether this machine can carry the project's messages now: the transport installed and the
  # conversation set up. With detail when not.
  ready: bool,
  detail: string,
  # True for an offline project: its conversation must be on this machine's loopback.
  loopbackOnly: bool,
  # The person's login this account goes by on the transport, as the transport kept it; "" before the
  # first setup. It is what this account's accounts there are named after. Named machine from before a
  # login took a machine's name's place.
  machine: string,
  # Whether this machine is in the conversation. False on a shared server until a person there has let
  # this machine's account in; while it is, no task of the project starts, and 'detail' says whom to let in.
  admitted: bool,
  # This machine's account there, as the server names it, and the conversation as a person names it: what
  # a person needs to let it in. Plain, not secrets; "" when the transport does not say.
  address: string,
  room: string,
  # Why nothing goes in or out of it now, for a person to act on, or "": "the vault is locked", for
  # one, where the project's and its tasks' accounts cannot be read until it is unlocked.
  waits: string,
  # What it waits on, for an interface to act on: "vault" while the vault is locked, else "". More values may come; an unknown one is shown as 'waits' alone.
  waitsOn: string,
  # Path of the vault file this daemon checked while waitsOn is "vault", "" otherwise.
  # This account's file on the daemon's machine, never a host name or a remote machine's vault.
  vault: string
)

# A connection a task tried to make and was stopped for.
#
# This is the one event with a deadline. The task is blocked while it waits, and the watcher
# gives up after its own timeout - so an interface that shows these must show them as they
# arrive, which is why Prompts is a stream and not a poll.
#
# The answer comes back on the same stream: the same destination again, with verdict set. An
# interface should match it to the question by task and key rather than showing it twice.
type Prompt (
  # Container name, for routing the answer back to the right task.
  task: string,
  # Pass this to Decide unchanged. It is derived from the other fields, but the derivation is
  # the daemon's and a client that rebuilt it would answer a prompt that does not exist.
  key: string,
  # Host or address the task asked for.
  destination: string,
  # tcp or udp.
  protocol: string,
  port: int,
  # When it was blocked, ISO-8601 - and on an event carrying a verdict, when it was decided.
  # The two are the same field because they are the same event twice; keep the question's own
  # value if you want the block time of an answered prompt.
  at: string,
  # Where in the ruleset it was stopped, for an operator who needs to know which rule fired.
  # Empty on an event carrying a verdict: nothing decided it a second time.
  prefix: string,
  # When the watcher gives up, ISO-8601, on an open question. "" when it never will, and "" on
  # an event carrying a verdict - nothing is waiting any more.
  #
  # A moment rather than a length, and computed where the timeout lives: an interface showing
  # "three minutes left" would otherwise be doing arithmetic on a number nobody sent it. It is
  # measured from when the question was asked rather than from "at", because "at" is when the
  # connection was blocked and a reader that has fallen behind would publish a deadline already
  # in the past.
  #
  # It belongs to the question, not to the packet. A dropped connection is retried, so the same
  # destination arrives again while one question is open - "at" moves with each, the deadline does
  # not. Recomputing it per event would hand a countdown a fresh minute every retry and it would
  # never visibly run out.
  deadline: string,
  # A destination is announced as open at most once. Its connection is retried and the watcher
  # sees every retry, but a key it has already answered is not published again - so a client that
  # took a settled question off screen never has it put back with buttons under it.
  #
  # Absent while the question is open. Once it is settled the same destination arrives again
  # carrying "allow", "deny" or "timeout", and that is the only way an interface learns that a
  # prompt ran out: nothing will ask about it again, so a question that simply stops arriving is
  # indistinguishable from one still waiting for its operator.
  verdict: ?string,
  # How many events were dropped since the previous one on this stream, because it was read more
  # slowly than they arrived. Absent when none were.
  #
  # A dropped question is not asked again, so its task stays blocked until its deadline with
  # nothing on screen. A client that sees this should tell its operator that questions may be
  # waiting unseen, rather than go on as if it had seen everything.
  missed: ?int
)

# One key a host offers, as this machine saw it.
type HostKey (
  # The algorithm, as ssh names it.
  type: string,
  # SHA256:... - the form ssh itself prints, and the form a person is told out of band.
  fingerprint: string
)

type DeleteOutcome (
  # Everything Porelo built for it is gone.
  DELETED,
  # What would go, having removed nothing.
  PREVIEWED,
  # Refused: work reached the gate and nobody reviewed it. The same name Stop uses, for the same
  # situation, so it can read the same on a screen.
  HOLDS_WORK,
  # Refused: tasks are still up.
  TASKS_RUNNING,
  # Nothing here knows that project.
  NO_SUCH_PROJECT,
  # Something could not be removed. detail says what.
  FAILED
)

# Something wrong with one answer.
type Problem (
  # Which answer, named as the call named it.
  field: string,
  what: string,
  # Whether it stops the project being created. A non-fatal one is worth showing and not worth
  # blocking on - a base image that is not on the machine yet will simply be pulled.
  fatal: bool
)

type TalkEvent (
  # Container name of the task whose mailbox this is about.
  task: string,
  # When it happened, RFC 3339.
  at: string,
  # taken, queued, sent, deferred, held, delivered, duplicate, refused (by a person), overridden
  # (delivered by a person although the filter refused it) or read (what the task sent was read where it
  # went, as a transport that confirms reading says; detail gives when).
  event: string,
  # The message's file name.
  message: string,
  # Its message id, or "" when none could be read.
  id: string,
  # The peer it was going to or came from, or "".
  peer: string,
  # Why, for the events that have a why. "" otherwise.
  detail: string
)

# The project has no conversation: no peer is reached through a transport's own ('<scheme>:').
error NoConversation(project: string)

# A credential somebody has to authorize.
type AuthorizationNeeded (
  # The vault entry that names the service; pass it to Authorize as 'name'.
  credential: string,
  # The task that needed it, or "".
  task: string,
  # That task's project, or "".
  project: string,
  # never, ended or granted.
  state: string,
  # When it was raised - or, for granted, when the grant was seen - ISO 8601.
  at: string
)

# The name is not a destination declared on this machine. From Destination, 'name' is the destination asked
# for. From Start, it is the CREDENTIAL whose destination is not declared - the entry the project or the run
# named, which is what the person corrects - and never the destination's name.
error NoSuchDestination(name: string)

# One thing a clearing handled: of a project, the account or the vault.
type ClearedItem (
  # task, mirror, image, conversation, transport, deploy key, signer, vault, keyring, grant and the
  # like.
  kind: string,
  # Which one, for a person.
  what: string,
  # REMOVED, WOULD_REMOVE, NOT_REMOVED, or FOR_A_PERSON: only a person's credentials can remove it.
  status: string,
  # Why, where it was not removed or is left for a person; "" otherwise.
  why: string,
  # The transport whose account of a project it is, for a reader that acts on it; "" for anything else.
  transport: string,
  # The project whose account it is; "" for anything not a project's account.
  project: string
)

# A deploy key this machine made for one repository: the public half, for a person or an interface to
# register at the forge. The secret half never leaves this machine's vault.
type MachineDeployKey (
  # Its vault entry.
  entry: string,
  repository: string,
  # The repository's upstream it is declared for.
  upstream: string,
  # 'ssh-ed25519 AAAA... porelo@<host>'.
  publicKey: string,
  # 'SHA256:...'.
  fingerprint: string,
  # What the forge should call it: 'porelo <machine> <project>/<repository>'.
  title: string,
  # Whether to register it with write access. False for the project's own repository, which a machine
  # reads and never writes; true for a work repository, where approved work is pushed.
  writeAccess: bool,
  # Whether it was made just now rather than found.
  made: bool
)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- errors

# The method only works as a stream and was called without "more".
error StreamRequired(method: string)

error ProjectRequired()

# Anything else that went wrong. The message is for a person, not for a client to match on.
error Failed(message: string)

# What the work in a task is doing, as against what its container is doing.
#
# "running" answers the container's question. This answers the one an operator has, and they are
# not the same: a task blocked on a question nobody saw and a task grinding through a build are
# both running: true, and that difference is the one that costs an afternoon.
type Activity (
  # Its container is not up. Stopped, finished or dead - all three are gone.
  DEAD,
  # Waiting for a person to answer something, said by the thing that asked rather than guessed
  # from how long it has been quiet. waitingFor says what about.
  WAITING,
  # Producing output.
  WORKING,
  # Up and quiet, with no recorded question for a person. This does not mean "waiting for a message";
  # silence cannot say that. inboxWaiting counts what is waiting, for a client to interpret.
  IDLE,
  # Up, and its agent's run has ended - finished, or stopped by an error its agent read from its own
  # output. agentEnded says how. Neither working nor idle: nothing will happen any more on its own.
  ENDED,
  # Up, and nothing on this side can see what it is doing - a task somebody attached a terminal
  # to writes its work to that terminal. Its own value rather than "idle", because a state that
  # is silently wrong is worse than one that says it does not know.
  UNKNOWN
)

# How a task's agent ended its run, as the agent read its own last event.
type AgentEnded (
  # When the task's log was last written, ISO-8601.
  at: string,
  # Whether the prompt was answered to its end.
  finished: bool,
  # "" when finished; else the text as the agent or its provider gave it.
  error: string,
  # Whose the error is: "provider" or "agent". A provider's is named as the provider's, so a person
  # looks there - credits, key, limits - and not at Porelo.
  source: string,
  # The provider's HTTP status, when the provider refused: 402, 401, 429, 403.
  status: ?int,
  # Changed files and commits in the workspace that never reached the gate: work an agent wrote
  # before it ended and did not push.
  unpushed: int
)

# How a person is meant to be involved in a task.
type Mode (
  # A terminal in the container, driven by hand.
  SHELL,
  # The agent's own session, attached, with a person working through it.
  AGENT,
  # Started with a prompt and left to run. Nobody is expected to be watching.
  UNATTENDED
)

# What Start would do to one task, answered before anybody presses it.
#
# An interface names its action by this and shows a refusal as unavailable with its reason, rather
# than letting somebody find out by pressing. Answered per task in List and Watch rather than by a
# call per row: an interface drawing forty tiles would otherwise ask forty times for something the
# listing already knew.
#
# Distinct from StartOutcome, which answers "can work begin in this PROJECT at all" - agent,
# provider, credential. This answers "what happens to THIS task". A client MUST render a value it
# does not know rather than failing on it; this enumeration will gain entries.
type StartAction (
  # Nothing exists under this name. Start creates the container and starts it.
  CREATE,
  # It exists and is stopped. Start brings it back with the workspace, the branch and the
  # uncommitted changes it already has.
  RESUME,
  # It is already running. Start refuses rather than starting a second one; Attach is what
  # somebody wants.
  RUNNING,
  # It would be resumed, but the gate token it has to come back holding is in a vault that is
  # locked. The per-task face of StartOutcome's VAULT_LOCKED, and for the same reason: the one
  # thing that helps is unlocking, so an interface offers that instead of reporting a failure.
  NEEDS_VAULT,
  # Its name carries a run id from before one container per task, so nothing maps it to a task and
  # nothing starts it. Remove is what applies. 'startDetail' names the task it belonged to.
  SUPERSEDED_NAME,
  # It was started before this machine restarted. Its sockets were under the runtime directory,
  # which the system clears on restart, and the container is bound to one of them, so Start
  # refuses. The workspace is still inside the container: 'startDetail' says how to copy it out
  # before Remove.
  PREDATES_RESTART,
  # Something not specific to this task is in the way - project file, agent, provider or
  # credential. 'startDetail' carries the StartOutcome value naming which.
  NOT_READY
)

# One task on this machine, running or not.
#
# A task is a container plus the helpers Porelo started beside it. Both halves matter: a
# container that is up with no helpers has lost its gate or its clearance watcher, and is not
# the same thing as a healthy task.
# What an agent's screen says about waiting for a person. See Task.screen.
type ScreenState (WAITING, NOT_WAITING, UNDECLARED, UNSEEN)

# Whether a finished run's last message was a question. See Task.asked.
type Asked (ASKED, DID_NOT_ASK, CANNOT_SAY)

type Task (
  # Container name, which is what every other method takes as its task parameter.
  name: string,
  # Project the task belongs to, or "" when nothing recorded it.
  project: string,
  # Which of the project's repositories this task works on.
  #
  # "" for a container created before repositories existed. That is a task on the project's own
  # repository - the only one there was - and is to be rendered as such, NOT as "unknown".
  #
  # A task works on exactly one repository, fixed when the task was created. Carry it over when
  # offering to start a stopped task again, and pass it back as Start's 'repository'.
  repository: string,
  # The commit this task's configuration was verified at, or "" when nothing verified it.
  #
  # A task of an organization's project runs against the file its start judged, and that file moves on.
  # "What was this task actually running under" is asked after something went wrong, so it is kept
  # with the task rather than read back out of the project.
  commit: string,
  # offline, guarded or online - or "" when nothing recorded it.
  securityClass: string,
  # The container runtime's own words, such as "Up 4 minutes" or "Exited (143) 2 seconds ago".
  # Free text meant for a person; do not parse it. Use running for the decision.
  state: string,
  # Whether the runtime says it is up.
  running: bool,
  # How many recorded helper processes are alive.
  helpers: int,
  # Agent running in it, by the name Agents reports. "" when it has none, or when the task was
  # started by a Porelo that did not record it.
  agent: string,
  # The model provider its agent was brokered to, by the name Providers reports. "" when it was
  # brokered to none, or when the task was started by a Porelo that did not record it.
  provider: string,
  # How somebody is meant to be involved: SHELL, AGENT or UNATTENDED, spelled as Mode spells
  # them. A string rather than the Mode type because it has a fourth state the type does not: ""
  # for a task started before this field existed, where nothing recorded one and inventing an
  # answer would be worse than admitting there is none.
  mode: string,
  # What an unattended task was asked to do. Kept after it has finished, because "what was this
  # asked to do" is a question people have about a task that is over. "" for the other modes.
  prompt: string,
  # Ref its work goes to, such as refs/porelo/incoming/shell. "" when it has no gate.
  branch: string,
  # The branch its work carries beyond the gate, as its start settled it from the project's branch
  # scheme or --branch: what an online task's push arrives as at the forge, and where a guarded
  # task's approval goes by default in a project with a branch block. porelo/<task> for a task
  # started before it was recorded; "" when it has no gate.
  forgeBranch: string,
  # Whether its commits are signed by a key of its own, certified at its start by a person the
  # project's access file admits, and judged at its gate when they arrive. false for a task whose
  # starter's agent held no such key, and for every task started before this field existed.
  certified: bool,
  # When its current certificate ends, ISO-8601 in UTC, renewed by 'porelo task certify'. "" when it
  # has none, or for a task started before this field existed.
  certifiedUntil: string,
  # When the current state began, ISO-8601: a running task since it started, a stopped one since
  # it exited. "" when the runtime could not say - a container that was created and never started
  # has no honest answer. This is what "idle for forty minutes" is arithmetic on; state is not.
  since: string,
  # What the work is doing, beside state rather than instead of it.
  activity: Activity,
  # Last modification time of task.log, as an ISO-8601 instant; "" without a readable log.
  # Ask Tail(last: 1) for the last line when showing it. This timestamp alone does not change Watch.
  lastWrote: string,
  # Number of .json files in the host-side inbox/new, 0 without a readable mailbox.
  # Reported for running, ended and stopped tasks alike; a change does change Watch.
  inboxWaiting: int,
  # How its agent's run ended: while activity is ENDED, and for a stopped task whose container
  # stopped with its run (activity DEAD). Absent while it runs, and for an agent that does not read
  # its own end.
  agentEnded: ?AgentEnded,
  # What it is waiting to be told, when activity is WAITING. "" otherwise.
  waitingFor: string,
  # What this task does with a blocked connection: "prompt", "allow", "deny" or "off" - the value
  # Start was given. "" when nothing recorded it, which is every task started before this field
  # existed.
  #
  # "off" is the one to mark wherever the task appears: nothing asks and nothing is refused, so
  # the firewall is loaded and no decision is ever put to anybody. It is a state somebody chose,
  # and an interface that shows it like any other run is hiding the choice.
  clearance: string,
  # A changeable caption somebody gave this task. "" when nobody has, which is every task by
  # default and is a normal state rather than a gap.
  #
  # BESIDE the name, never instead of it: 'name' is what every other method takes and what
  # somebody types into porelo at the machine, so a row that showed only this would make the
  # interface and the command line disagree about what a thing is called. Absent and empty are
  # deliberately the same state.
  label: string,
  # Whether this task's own work is waiting at the gate. 1 when its ref is waiting for review, 0
  # otherwise - and 0 for an online project, where nothing is ever reviewed, which is not a
  # smaller number but a question the class does not have.
  #
  # Answered here rather than left to a join, because there is no join to make: 'name' is a
  # CONTAINER name (porelo-<project>-<task>-<run>), PendingPush.name is a TASK name, and several
  # containers over time share one ref. Anything lined up from the two would be right for at most
  # one of them and silently wrong for the rest.
  waiting: int,
  # Name within the project, which is what Start takes. 'name' is the container it produced.
  #
  # Carried rather than derived: an interface must not cut a prefix off 'name' to get it, because
  # the two are related only by a rule Porelo owns and may change - it changed in this release. ""
  # for a container from before this field existed, where nothing recorded one.
  task: string,
  # What Start would do to this task, without doing it.
  startAction: StartAction,
  # Which refusal, when startAction is one: the StartOutcome value for NOT_READY, the task a
  # SUPERSEDED_NAME belonged to, free text otherwise. For RESUME, "" - or, for a task the
  # machine's restart took down, "the machine restarted; starting it brings it back whole", so an
  # interface can say why it is down without reading a boot log. For one started with a prompt and left
  # to run, "the machine's restart interrupted its run; it stays stopped with its session kept, and its
  # next start continues it": nothing starts it on its own. "" otherwise.
  startDetail: string,
  # What this task is doing that takes minutes and has no other visible sign, such as "building".
  # "" when it is in none.
  #
  # Here rather than only in Start's stream, because a Start given 'now' returns at once and a
  # stream tied to the call that started it dies with that call. A window opened a minute later, or
  # a second machine watching the same daemon, would otherwise see a task with nothing behind it.
  # The output itself is read with Tail.
  phase: string,
  # Whether the agent's own screen shows it waiting for a person - DERIVED from a rule the agent's
  # package declares, read from the screen tmux draws in an attached task. Never observed, and kept
  # apart from activity and waitingFor, which are: render it as a reading, not a fact.
  #
  # WAITING      a declared rule matched
  # NOT_WAITING  the agent declares rules and none matched - quiet is not waiting
  # UNDECLARED   the agent declares nothing for its screen, so nothing can be said. Not NOT_WAITING:
  #              "this agent cannot tell us" is honest, "not waiting" would not be
  # UNSEEN       nothing here can see its screen: not attached, not running, or a pager is over it
  screen: ScreenState,
  # What it waits for, when the rule that matched says. "" otherwise.
  screenWaitingFor: string,
  # The agent declares rules, its screen has been read for a day, and none has ever matched: more
  # likely a declaration its current version no longer fits than an agent that never waits.
  screenUnproven: bool,
  # What its last unattended run said last, from the run's own records, where the agent declares
  # which record that is. Observed, not derived. "" when it does not say, or said nothing yet. The
  # run's next start replaces it.
  lastMessage: string,
  # Whether that last message put something to the person. DERIVED, and three-valued on purpose:
  # CANNOT_SAY is what every agent answers until one can declare that it stopped to ask - a run that
  # ended with a question looks exactly like one that finished.
  asked: Asked,
  # Where asked came from: "declared" for a rule the agent declares, "" for none.
  askedFrom: string,
  # The session its agent was running, recorded where the agent declares how to name one - from an
  # unattended run's records, or an attached agent's own session files when the task stops. Starting
  # the task again continues it, and says so. "" when none is recorded: the next start is a fresh
  # session. An identifier, not a credential; gone when the task is removed.
  session: string,
  # What this task was given beyond its agent's own credential, from the project and its run: a vault
  # entry's name to the destination it is for. Names only, never a token.
  credentials: [string]string,
  # For each of those credentials a person granted: who and when, as it stood when the task started - the
  # person this task acts as. Empty when none is a grant.
  grants: [string]Authorization,
  # The values it was started with, by the name project.yml declares under 'parameters:', each with where
  # it came from. Fixed for the task's life, as its environment is. Empty when it was started with none.
  parameters: [string]ParameterValue,
  # The container this task is: the id Porelo recorded when it was created. A task's name is the next free one
  # in its project, so after a remove a later task can carry the same name; HandIns tells their records apart
  # by this. "" for a task with none recorded.
  run: string,
  # The bytes one file handed to this task may have: the project's limits.hand_in when it started (64 MiB
  # unless the project file says otherwise), fixed for the task's life like its other limits. A client
  # refuses a larger file before reading it; HandIn refuses it too, as FileTooLarge.
  handInLimit: int,
  # What is in /porelo/files now, as this run was handed it, oldest first. Empty when nothing was.
  files: []HandedFile,
  # What the builds of this run's pushes did, the newest push first, each with its newest verdict. Empty outside
  # an online project that names a forge under builds, and before the first push. A helper on the host follows
  # every commit the task pushes; the task asks for nothing and holds no forge credential.
  builds: []Build,
  # The forge whose builds of this task's pushes are followed, as the project names it under builds.forge
  # ("github"); "" when the project names none, or the task is not online. With builds empty and buildProblem
  # empty, it means no push yet.
  buildReader: string,
  # Why the named reader does not follow them: not installed, would not start, speaks another version of the
  # build protocol, the task's launch stopped before the helper that follows them, or that helper ended while the
  # task runs. "" while it follows them, and when buildReader is "". What goes wrong while it follows - a
  # rate limit, a refused credential, the vault shut - is each Build's detail instead.
  buildProblem: string
)

# The build of one commit the task pushed, as Porelo handed it into the task: build-<first 12 of commit>.txt in
# /porelo/files, and each delivered log beside it. A log's text is in the task only.
type Build (
  commit: string,
  # "queued" | "running" | "success" | "failure" | "cancelled" | "unknown"
  verdict: string,
  # Every job the forge reports for the commit, in its order, once the verdict is "failure" or final: with the
  # project's builds.logs "failure" (the default) a log for each failed job, with "all" a log for every job once
  # the verdict is final. Empty while queued or running, and for "unknown". At most 50.
  jobs: []BuildJob,
  # UTC, when the verdict last changed.
  since: string,
  # Why "unknown" - a rate limit, a credential the forge refused, the vault shut, no build for that commit, no
  # verdict within the deadline - or what more the forge said; empty otherwise.
  detail: string
)

# One job of a build.
type BuildJob (
  # As the forge spells it, workflow and job: "Build / unit tests".
  name: string,
  # What became of it, as the forge says it, lower case: "success" | "failure" | "cancelled" | "skipped" | ...;
  # while it is not finished, its state: "queued" | "in_progress" | ... A client shows one it does not know as it
  # comes.
  result: string,
  # Its log's name in /porelo/files, the log's last 64 KiB: build-<first 12 of commit>-<n>.log, n counting from 1 in
  # this list; empty when no log of it was delivered.
  log: string
)

# One file handed to a running task, as the record holds it.
type HandedFile (
  # As it appears in /porelo/files.
  name: string,
  bytes: int,
  # Of the content, lower-case hex.
  sha256: string,
  # UTC, when it was complete in the task.
  at: string,
  # Who handed it in: the account the call came from, or "porelo" for what Porelo hands in itself (a build's
  # verdict and log). A client compares against "porelo", never guesses from the file's name.
  by: string,
  # The task's run it went to: Task.run then.
  run: string
)

# One line of a task's hand-in record.
type HandIn (
  # "given", "replaced" (a file of that name was there and is replaced) or "taken back".
  event: string,
  file: HandedFile
)

# What became of a Stop or a Remove.
#
# One enumeration serves both because one enumeration implements both. A client should render
# every value it can receive: a refusal is the interesting outcome, not an error case.
type Outcome (
  # Stop: the name is not one Porelo created. Nothing was touched.
  NOT_A_TASK,
  # Stop: no container and no helpers by that name.
  NOTHING_TO_STOP,
  # Stop: the container is down and the helpers are gone.
  STOPPED,
  # Stop: refused, because the task holds commits that never reached the gate. work says what.
  # Re-ask Remove with force to discard it, or with rescue to push it into the mirror first.
  HOLDS_WORK,
  # Stop: nothing could say whether there was work, so it was not removed.
  NOTHING_KNOWS,
  # Stop: rescue was asked for, but the container is already down and cannot be read.
  RESCUE_NEEDS_IT_RUNNING,
  # Stop: rescue was attempted and failed. detail says why. Nothing was removed.
  RESCUE_FAILED,
  # Remove: the container was removed and its helpers were already gone.
  REMOVED,
  # Remove: refused because the task is running. Stop it, or ask again with force.
  STILL_RUNNING
)

# One log a task has written.
type Log (
  # Pass this to Tail unchanged.
  name: string,
  # How large it is now.
  bytes: int,
  # When it was last written, ISO-8601.
  at: string,
  # What this log holds, for a name that does not say - absent where it does.
  #
  # Two files need it and they are the two somebody looks for: 'events.jsonl' is what the firewall
  # stopped, and 'reader.err' is the process that records those complaining. A table of names at
  # the client end would drift the day a file is added - the list grows, the new one has no
  # sentence, and it looks like the ordinary case. Only this side knows which files exist.
  #
  # Absent, empty and whitespace all mean the same thing: nothing to say. Sent absent rather than
  # empty, because an empty line under a name reads as a description that failed.
  what: ?string
)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- what exists

# Every task on this machine, running or stopped.
#
# unrecognized names the containers named like a task that are not one: Porelo holds no container id for them
# (made before it recorded one, or by something else), so no method acts on them and none adopts them. A client
# shows them as what they are, never as tasks: removing one removes what is in it.
method List() -> (tasks: []Task, unrecognized: []string)

# The same answer, again whenever it changes.
#
# Called without "more" this answers once, exactly as List does, so a client that cannot
# stream is not locked out of the data.
#
# Called with "more" it sends the whole list on every change and nothing in between. The age
# in state is deliberately not a change: it moves on its own, and a view that redrew for it
# would redraw every second forever.
method Watch() -> (tasks: []Task, unrecognized: []string)

# The purposes a credential may serve: "git" and "any". "git" is the only one anything reads
# today; a record with another is kept and used by nothing. When a second consumer appears it will
# be named here.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- running tasks

# One step of a start, as the start takes it. The interface shows text, waitingOn and cause, and writes none of its own.
type StartStep (
  # Stable name: SOURCE, CHECKS, CREDENTIALS, WORKSPACE, CONVERSATION, IMAGE, CONTAINER, HELPERS or AGENT.
  # A later release may add one; show an unknown one by its text.
  step: string,
  # STARTED, WAITING, DONE or FAILED. An unknown one is shown by its text.
  state: string,
  # One English sentence, such as "Joining the project's conversation on matrix".
  text: string,
  # What it waits on, such as "the room's homeserver"; "" when it waits on nothing outside Porelo.
  waitingOn: string,
  # FAILED only: the dependency's last error line, at most 300 characters; "" otherwise.
  cause: string
)

# Starts a task: creates one that is not there, or brings back one that is stopped.
#
# One verb, because the caller cannot know which case they are in - and pressing is not how
# somebody should find out. That is what startAction on the listing is for. A task that is already
# running is refused rather than started twice.
#
# With "more" it streams lines as they happen; steps: true adds step-only replies. An interface should show
# them: building an image takes minutes, and showing nothing for that long is indistinguishable
# from having hung. With "now" it returns as soon as the task exists and the build goes on without
# it; the task is in List and Watch immediately with its phase set, and Tail reads the output.
#
# The task outlives this call and this connection either way. Closing the socket mid-build does not
# cancel it; List shows it afterwards.
method Start(
  # Task name within the project. Omitted, the repository's name, or the next free one (my-first-2), never an
  # existing task's.
  task: ?string,
  # Project NAME, as Projects.name gives it. REQUIRED - there is no default directory any more.
  project: ?string,
  # Agent to run, by the name Agents reports. Default: the agent the project file names for the
  # repository (Repository.agent), else the one found signed in on this machine, else the only one
  # installed, or an error naming them all when that does not decide.
  agent: ?string,
  # Provider to route through. Default: the one a chosen agent was found signed in to, else the
  # agent's own declared default, or an error when it declares none. A project file may name a
  # repository's agent, never a provider.
  provider: ?string,
  # Overrides the stored credential kind for this run.
  credentialType: ?string,
  # Lifetime of the gate token. Default: 8.
  tokenHours: ?int,
  # Upstream to gate against. Default: the project's own.
  upstream: ?string,
  # Which of the project's repositories this task works on. REQUIRED, and there is no default.
  #
  # A project is a unit of work over one or more repositories, and a task works on exactly one of
  # them. The project's own repository - where project.yml, the planning and the issues live - is
  # one of the choices, and is how an agent gets a task for planning. Project.repositories lists
  # what to offer, its own first.
  #
  # Not defaulted even when a project has exactly one. A default would be paid for later and
  # somewhere else: a project that grew a second repository would silently change what an existing
  # call does. Omitting it is refused, and the refusal names what there is to choose from.
  repository: ?string,
  # Credentials this task holds beyond the agent's own and the project's: a vault entry's name to the
  # destination or provider it is for. They add to the project's and cannot point one of its elsewhere.
  # Each reaches the task as PORELO_TOKEN_<ENTRY> and PORELO_URL_<ENTRY>, a token worthless anywhere else.
  credentials: ?[string]string,
  # Values for what project.yml declares under 'parameters:', by name. Each is checked against its
  # declaration before anything is made, a declared default fills one left out, and the task records each
  # with the origin the client gives ("client" when it gives none). Refused when a value is not allowed, names nothing declared, or a required one
  # is missing: CanStart answers PARAMETER_REFUSED. A task brought back keeps the values it was made with
  # and refuses new ones.
  parameters: ?[string]ParameterValue,
  # The task's branch at the forge, named outright: what an online task's push arrives as, and where a
  # guarded task's approval goes by default in a project with a branch block. Omitted, project.yml's
  # branch.template makes it (porelo/<task> without one). It must match branch.pattern; CanStart answers
  # BRANCH_REFUSED for one that does not. Settled at the start and kept: a task brought back keeps it.
  branch: ?string,
  # Starting branch; a remote caller supplies its local checkout branch explicitly.
  # Otherwise base_branch in project.yml, then the repository default. A resume keeps its base.
  from: ?string,
  # Runs with no workspace and no git gate: the agent gets an empty directory and can commit
  # nowhere.
  noGate: ?bool,
  # Does everything up to starting the container, then stops and reports. Nothing is created.
  dryRun: ?bool,
  # What to do with a blocked connection: prompt, allow, deny or off. Default: "prompt".
  clearance: ?string,
  # Removes the container when the session ends. Default: false.
  #
  # REPLACES 'keep', whose default was the other way round: the command a person reaches for first
  # removed what it had just made, which is how an operator lost a task by typing the obvious
  # thing. Keeping is the safe default; throwing away is the deliberate one.
  rm: ?bool,
  # Returns as soon as the task exists, instead of waiting for the build to finish.
  #
  # Named for what it does rather than 'detach': over this interface Start never attaches anything,
  # so there is nothing to detach from. The command line has a second switch for not attaching a
  # terminal, which is a question only a terminal has.
  now: ?bool,
  # How somebody is meant to be involved, recorded on the task and answered by List and Watch.
  # Default: UNATTENDED when a prompt is given, SHELL otherwise - so a caller that gives a prompt
  # need not repeat itself, and one that gives neither gets the honest answer for a container
  # nobody is attached to.
  mode: ?Mode,
  # What an unattended task is asked to do. Recorded on the task and kept after it finishes.
  #
  # Giving one makes this call RUN the agent, not merely start the container: the call lasts as
  # long as the run does, and with "more" the agent's own output arrives as it is produced. The
  # exit code in the reply is then the agent's. Without a prompt the container comes up and the
  # call returns, as it always did.
  prompt: ?string,
  # Model to ask the agent for, when it declares a model flag. Ignored otherwise.
  model: ?string,
  # Turn limit, when the agent declares one.
  maxTurns: ?int,
  # The most subagents the agent may run at the same time inside the task; 0 for none. Default: the
  # project file's limits.subagents, else 0. A number the agent's definition says it cannot enforce
  # is refused with exitCode 64 before anything is made, and so is anything but a whole number from
  # 0 to 2147483647 (a fraction, a string, a boolean); CanStart answers SUBAGENTS_REFUSED for the
  # project file's number. A task brought back keeps its number and hands it to its agent again: the
  # same number is taken, another is refused with exitCode 64.
  subagents: ?int,
  # How long the agent may run, in minutes. Default: 30. On expiry the run is killed and whatever
  # it produced is kept - the exit code is 124 and the log holds what it managed to say.
  minutes: ?int,
  # Asks the person's own computer for the task's certificate, on a stream only: one continuing reply
  # may carry certify, answered by Certify on another connection. An interface sends it for a machine
  # that is not its own, as a host starts no task uncertified and holds no person's agent.
  # A start in default on a machine that is no host asks nothing, as nothing there needs a certificate.
  certify: ?bool,
  # Streams each step as its own reply, carrying step and no line. Default false: the stream is lines only, as before.
  steps: ?bool
) -> (
  # One line of output. Set on every continuing reply unless steps: true was requested.
  line: ?string,
  # One step, on a streamed reply only with steps: true. A reply carries line or step, never both.
  step: ?StartStep,
  # What a certify: true start asks the person's computer to sign. Answer it by Certify. A reply
  # carrying it carries nothing else.
  certify: ?CertifyRequest,
  # Every step, on the final reply when not streamed. Absent when streamed.
  steps: ?[]StartStep,
  # Container name of the started task. Set on the final reply, and only then.
  container: ?string,
  # Starting branch and source, once settled; independent of the task forge branch.
  baseBranch: ?string,
  # CHECKOUT, FROM, PROJECT or DEFAULT.
  baseSource: ?string,
  # What the launch returned. 0 is success. Set on the final reply.
  exitCode: ?int,
  # Everything that was printed, for a caller that did not ask to stream. Empty when it did.
  output: ?[]string,
  # What it did, or the refusal it answered with. Set on the final reply: CREATE or RESUME for what
  # it did; RUNNING, NEEDS_VAULT or PREDATES_RESTART for a refusal.
  #
  # A refusal is an ordinary reply, not an error, and carries container and exitCode like any
  # other - the exit code is the refusal's. NEEDS_VAULT in particular arrives this way, so an
  # interface can offer to unlock the vault rather than report a failure.
  action: ?StartAction,
  # How many recorded helpers were started, and how many the task is recorded as having had. Fewer
  # started than recorded is a partial start and an interface should say so. Absent when the task
  # was created rather than resumed - 0 of 0 would claim an answer to a question nobody asked.
  helpersStarted: ?int,
  helpersRecorded: ?int,
  # What went wrong per helper, for the ones that did not come back.
  problems: ?[]string,
  # Set when the image the container was built from has changed since it was created.
  imageDrift: ?string
)

# What a start asks a person's computer to certify: the key of the one principal, until the end.
type CertifyRequest (
  # The id of this one wait, for Certify.
  start: string,
  # The task's public key, ssh-ed25519 AAAA...
  key: string,
  # The one principal the certificate names, task:<project>:<task>.
  principal: string,
  # The certificate's key id.
  key_id: string,
  # When it ends, ISO-8601 in UTC.
  valid_before: string,
  # The keys that may certify; empty for a task with no project, which its starter's key certifies.
  certifiers: []string
)

# Answers a start waiting for its certificate, with the certificate or with the words it ends with,
# one of them. What comes back is checked as any other, and the start refuses what does not hold. A
# start waits five minutes, or until its stream closes. An id no start waits by is NoSuchStart.
method Certify(start: string, certificate: ?string, refused: ?string) -> ()

# Brings a task's repository at the gate up to its source - the checkout it was started in, or its remote - and,
# when a branch moved, tells its agent through its mailbox, so 'git fetch porelo' in the task brings it. Starting a
# stopped task does the same on its own. outcome: MOVED (moved names each branch that moved and the commit it names
# now; told says whether the agent heard), UNCHANGED, NOT_GATED (an online task, which fetches its upstream itself),
# FAILED (detail says why). A name that is no task here is NoSuchTask.
method RefreshTask(task: string) -> (outcome: string, moved: [string]string, detail: string, told: bool)

# Stops a task and the helpers Porelo started beside it.
#
# The container is stopped, NOT removed: its filesystem is the task's workspace, so removing it
# would throw away the agent's work. Removal is Remove's decision, and asking for it here is what
# purge used to do.
#
# On the way down it writes what the task holds, while that is still knowable - whoever removes it
# later cannot ask the container itself.
method Stop(
  # Container name.
  task: string
) -> (
  outcome: Outcome,
  # How many helper processes were stopped.
  helpers: int,
  # What is still alive afterwards: helpers and the container. Not zero is worth showing.
  surviving: int,
  # Why, in words, for an outcome that needs one. "" otherwise.
  detail: string
)

# Removes a task: its container, its state, and the workspace inside it.
#
# Refuses rather than destroys, in three ways, and an interface should render each as a question
# rather than an error:
#
#   STILL_RUNNING   it is up. Stop it first, or pass force.
#   HOLDS_WORK      it holds commits that never reached the gate. 'work' says what. Ask again with
#                   rescue to push them into the mirror first, or force to discard them.
#   NOTHING_KNOWS   nothing could say what it holds, so nothing was removed.
#
# The record NOTHING_KNOWS rests on is written by Stop. Where that record lives decides whether
# this guard survives a restart of the machine; see the note on the runtime directory.
method Remove(
  # Container name.
  task: string,
  # Push what it holds into the gate's mirror before removing it.
  rescue: ?bool,
  # Remove it anyway: stops it if it is running, and discards work that never reached the gate.
  # One flag for both refusals, deliberately - it is the "I mean it" answer, and splitting it would
  # invite passing whichever one happens to unblock this run without reading the other.
  force: ?bool
) -> (
  outcome: Outcome,
  # What it held, when that is why it was refused. "" otherwise.
  work: string,
  # Where rescued work was put. "" when none was.
  rescuedRef: string,
  # Whether the container was removed.
  removed: bool,
  # Why, in words, for an outcome that needs one. "" otherwise.
  detail: string,
  # How many paths the container had that its image did not - what the agent installed inside it,
  # which is destroyed with the container and has nowhere to arrive, unlike the workspace. Counted
  # only when it was removed, and zero otherwise. Show it beside the removal: nothing else records
  # that any of it existed.
  discarded: int
)

# One task a panic stopped, or would have stopped.
type PanickedTask (
  # Container name, which is what Start takes to bring it back.
  name: string,
  # How many helper processes it had.
  helpers: int,
  # Helpers, and the container, that outlived the stop and have to be stopped by hand. Empty is the
  # normal case.
  surviving: []string
)

# Stops every running task and every helper any of them started.
#
# No task names, on purpose: somebody reaching for this is not in a position to list what is
# running first, and a stop that needs an inventory is one they will get wrong.
#
# IT STOPS; IT NEVER REMOVES. Every workspace, log and unpushed commit survives, and Start brings
# a task back with the work it had. An interface that presented this as a cleanup would send
# somebody looking for work that is still exactly where it was. Discarding is a separate decision,
# made afterwards, by name, with Remove.
method Panic(
  # Lists what would be stopped and stops nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  # Every task that was running, in the order they were stopped. Empty means nothing was running.
  tasks: []PanickedTask,
  # Every helper and container that outlived its stop, across all tasks. Non-empty means somebody has
  # processes to stop by hand, and they are named rather than counted.
  surviving: []string,
  # Whether this only listed what would happen.
  previewed: bool,
  # The line written to the account's stop journal, stops.log: when, who, from where, what stopped and what
  # survived. Empty for a dry run, or when it could not be written - the stop happened all the same.
  journalled: string
)

# Drops the cached passphrase, device share and daemon-owned host share.
method Lock(
  # When supplied, must resolve to this daemon's vault.
  vault: ?string
) -> (
  # Whether the kernel keyring exists. A host share uses the daemon's locked memory instead.
  keyring: bool,
  # Whether a cached way in was held and has now been dropped. False means it was already locked.
  wasCached: bool,
  # Running tasks whose credential proxy already read the credential and holds it in its own
  # memory, where locking cannot reach. Stopping them is what ends that. An interface reporting
  # "locked" without this would be claiming more than happened.
  holding: int
)

# A value a task was started with, and where it came from: "command line", "file <path>", "environment",
# "default", or what a client of Start says, such as "form" - "client" when it says nothing.
type ParameterValue (
  value: string,
  origin: string
)

# Why work could not start here, or that it could.
#
# New values may appear; render an unrecognised one rather than throwing, by the same rule as
# every other enumeration here. The three that mean different ACTIONS are NO_PROVIDER_CHOSEN
# (pick a provider), CREDENTIAL_MISSING (store a secret) and VAULT_LOCKED (unlock it) - showing
# any of those as another is telling somebody to do the wrong thing.
type StartOutcome (
  # Nothing is in the way.
  READY,
  # The project file is not there, or cannot be read.
  NO_PROJECT_FILE,
  # The task name is one Start would refuse: lowercase letters, digits and inner hyphens, not
  # only digits, and 'porelo-<project>-<task>' at most 65 characters. 'detail' says which.
  BAD_TASK_NAME,
  # A value handed in is one project.yml's 'parameters:' does not allow, names no declaration, or a required
  # one is missing. 'detail' names it and what is allowed. Answered in every mode: no value nothing allows
  # reaches a task.
  PARAMETER_REFUSED,
  # The task's branch at the forge - named with 'branch', or made by project.yml's branch.template from
  # the task's name and values - is not one branch.pattern accepts or git can name, or the template names
  # a value the start does not give. 'detail' names the branch and the pattern.
  BRANCH_REFUSED,
  # No repository was named and Start never picks one, not even for a project that has exactly
  # one. Somebody chooses a REPOSITORY; Project.repositories lists them, its own first, and
  # 'detail' names them too. This is the outcome a start dialog turns into its required choice.
  NO_REPOSITORY_CHOSEN,
  # A repository was named and the project has none of that name. 'detail' lists what it has.
  UNKNOWN_REPOSITORY,
  # The chosen repository is reached over ssh at a host this machine never met. 'host' names it and
  # 'hostKeys' says what it offers now: a person confirms one with TrustHostKey
  # against what its owner publishes, then asks again. Before, the start went ahead without a gate.
  UNKNOWN_HOST_KEY,
  # The chosen repository's upstream was read as the gate's clone would read it, and could not be,
  # with no mirror yet for the task to start from. Asked last, once everything else is ready, because
  # it reaches the network. 'detail' says what was tried and ends with the command that fixes it.
  # With a mirror there, the start goes on from it and Start prints why among its output lines:
  # CanStart answers READY and does not ask the network. A successful read counts for 30 seconds,
  # so a dialog that asks again while a person chooses does not reach the upstream each time; a
  # refusal is never kept, so asking again after a fix sees it.
  # The host refused what was offered: no key it accepts, or a token it does not. 'detail' names
  # what was offered - nothing stored, or which vault entry.
  UPSTREAM_KEY_REFUSED,
  # The host answered and has no such repository, or none the credential may read.
  UPSTREAM_NOT_FOUND,
  # Nothing answered: no route, no such name, a timeout of ten seconds.
  UPSTREAM_UNREACHABLE,
  # Anything else; 'detail' carries git's own last line.
  UPSTREAM_UNREADABLE,
  # Nothing is installed to run. A task with no agent can still be started as a plain shell, so
  # this is not "the machine is broken".
  NO_AGENT,
  # A name was given and nothing is installed under it.
  UNKNOWN_AGENT,
  # Several are installed and none was named. Somebody chooses an agent.
  SEVERAL_AGENTS,
  # The agent names no default provider and none was chosen. Somebody chooses a PROVIDER - not a
  # secret, which is why this is not folded into CREDENTIAL_MISSING.
  NO_PROVIDER_CHOSEN,
  # The provider asked for is not declared on this machine.
  UNKNOWN_PROVIDER,
  # The provider is declared but does not serve the dialect this agent speaks.
  WRONG_DIALECT,
  # The vault cannot be read, so this question cannot be answered yet. NOT a missing credential:
  # the one thing that helps is unlocking, and telling somebody to store a secret they already
  # have is the instruction that cannot help them.
  VAULT_LOCKED,
  # The vault is readable and holds nothing under the name this run would look for. 'credential'
  # names it.
  CREDENTIAL_MISSING,
  # Something is stored and cannot be brokered - the wrong kind for this provider. 'detail' says
  # what.
  CREDENTIAL_UNUSABLE,
  # A credential the project or the run names is for a destination nobody declared here, or the run points
  # one of the project's elsewhere. 'credential' names it; 'detail' says whether the project or the run
  # named it. Start refuses the same case with NoSuchDestination, before anything is launched.
  UNKNOWN_DESTINATION,
  # A credential the project or the run names is one a person grants (oauth-device, oauth-code), and
  # nobody has granted it. 'credential' names the entry; 'detail' gives the command that grants it, and
  # Authorize is how an interface grants it. Start refuses an unattended or agent run for it; a shell
  # starts with a warning. A grant that ended at the service is not seen here: it is found when spent.
  AUTHORIZATION_NEEDED,
  # The start would have no gate (noGate), and the project declares host services, which a task
  # reaches at the gate's address. 'detail' names them. Start refuses the same case before anything is
  # created.
  HOST_SERVICES_NEED_A_GATE,
  # project.yml's limits.subagents, or the repository's, asks the agent for more subagents at the same
  # time than its definition says it can enforce. 'detail' names the agent and where the number came
  # from, in the words Start refuses with (exitCode 64).
  SUBAGENTS_REFUSED,
  # In 'default', the repository's porelo.yml, or the person's .porelo.yml beside it, sets something
  # other than the project does, outside the image, and this machine has not allowed exactly that.
  # settingsToAllow lists what is new, changed or removed since the last allowance (the first time,
  # all of it); settingsHash names the whole state asked for now. AllowSettings with that hash allows
  # it, and asking again answers READY until the files ask for something else. Start refuses the
  # same case, and asks in a terminal.
  SETTINGS_NOT_ALLOWED,
  # In an organization's project, a commit that changed the repository's porelo.yml is not signed by
  # a project admin or a developer with access to that repository. 'file' and 'commit' name it,
  # 'detail' says it in words; a history rewritten under the change this machine took last is refused
  # the same way, with 'commit' "". Nothing here can allow it: a person signs with their own commit.
  SETTINGS_UNSIGNED
)

# One setting a repository's porelo.yml asks for that this machine has not allowed.
type SettingToAllow (
  # Where it is, as a dotted path; a list entry as path[entry]: "egress.domains[repo.example.org]".
  key: string,
  # What it would be, as text to show. "" with removed.
  value: string,
  # What it was when last allowed, or what the project sets; "" when it is new.
  was: string,
  # Whether porelo.yml takes it away (null, "!entry" or "!*").
  removed: bool
)

# Something a later source took away from what the project sets, and which source did.
type RemovedSetting (
  # As SettingToAllow.key.
  key: string,
  # The source that removed it: "porelo.yml of <repository>" or "the local override".
  by: string
)

# Whether work could start here, asked before anything is created.
#
# This exists because the only other way to find out was to start a task and read an exit code and
# a stream of prose. It takes the same choices Start takes and answers from the same code, so a
# check that says READY and a Start that refuses cannot disagree.
#
# What it CANNOT tell you: whether the credential is still valid at the provider. It answers that
# something usable is stored under the name this run would look for, which is a different claim.
method CanStart(
  # Project NAME, as Start takes it. Omitted, no project is checked - which is how an interface
  # asks about the machine before a project has been chosen. A name this machine does not have is
  # an ANSWER here (NO_PROJECT_FILE, naming what it does have), not an error.
  project: ?string,
  # Which of the project's repositories the task would be for, exactly as Start takes it.
  # Omitted with a project given, the answer is NO_REPOSITORY_CHOSEN - because that is what Start
  # would do. Pass noGate when the run would have no gate: such a run needs none, and one named is
  # still refused as Start refuses it, before anything about its agent.
  repository: ?string,
  # Whether the run would have no workspace and no git gate, as Start takes it.
  noGate: ?bool,
  # The task name Start would be given. Omitted, no name is checked. The length rule needs the
  # project, so without one only the characters are.
  task: ?string,
  # Agent to run. Omitted, the agent the project file names for the repository, else the only one
  # installed.
  agent: ?string,
  # Provider to route through. Omitted, the agent's own declared default.
  provider: ?string,
  # Overrides the stored credential kind for this question, as Start takes it.
  credentialType: ?string,
  # The credentials the run would add, as Start takes them. Each the project or the run names must be for a
  # destination declared here, or the answer is UNKNOWN_DESTINATION.
  credentials: ?[string]string,
  # The values Start would be handed, as it takes them. One the project's declaration does not allow is
  # PARAMETER_REFUSED.
  parameters: ?[string]ParameterValue,
  # The task's branch at the forge, named outright, as Start takes it. Omitted, the one project.yml's
  # branch scheme makes. One its pattern refuses is BRANCH_REFUSED.
  branch: ?string
) -> (
  # Whether work could start. True exactly when outcome is READY, so branch on whichever suits -
  # but never infer the ACTION from this alone: three different things somebody has to do all
  # arrive as false.
  ready: bool,
  outcome: StartOutcome,
  # The agent this was answered for. "" when none was settled.
  agent: string,
  # The provider serving it. "" when the agent is not brokered, or none was settled.
  provider: string,
  # The vault key this run would look for. "" when the question never got that far.
  #
  # The name ACTUALLY looked for, not the one that ought to apply: a vault written before
  # credentials were keyed by provider still answers under the agent's own name, so this can be
  # either. Render this, not a name of your own composing - otherwise the sentence on screen names
  # a key that would be right in a vault the person does not have.
  credential: string,
  # What to run ON THAT MACHINE to store it, as arguments, with CREDENTIAL_MISSING. Empty
  # otherwise. Answered here rather than joined together from Providers() by a client: it is a
  # string this already builds, and a second place that builds it is a second place it can drift.
  storeCommand: []string,
  # What to run ON THAT MACHINE to copy what the agent already holds into the vault, as arguments,
  # with CREDENTIAL_MISSING for an agent found signed in by a route the import can take. Empty
  # otherwise. Offer it before storeCommand, since nothing has to be retyped.
  importCommand: []string,
  # Where the agent was named: FLAG, PROJECT (the repository's own agent in the project file),
  # SIGNED_IN (the one agent found signed in) or ONLY_ONE. "" when none was settled. Show an unknown
  # one as it comes.
  agentSource: string,
  # Where its sign-in was found, an absolute path on that machine. "" when none was.
  agentFoundIn: string,
  # With SEVERAL_AGENTS: the agents found signed in, for a choice to put first. No client reads
  # detail for them.
  signedIn: []string,
  # Prose for a person, always set. Never parsed.
  detail: string,
  # With UNKNOWN_HOST_KEY: the host, spelled exactly as TrustHostKey wants it - with its port off
  # port 22. "" otherwise.
  host: string,
  # With UNKNOWN_HOST_KEY: what that host offers right now. Empty otherwise.
  hostKeys: []HostKey,
  # With SETTINGS_NOT_ALLOWED: what is to be allowed, in the order porelo.yml writes it. Empty
  # otherwise.
  settingsToAllow: []SettingToAllow,
  # With SETTINGS_NOT_ALLOWED: the SHA-256, hex, over settingsToAllow exactly as answered; what
  # AllowSettings takes. "" otherwise.
  settingsHash: string,
  # With SETTINGS_UNSIGNED: the file, relative to the repository ("porelo.yml"). "" otherwise.
  file: string,
  # With SETTINGS_UNSIGNED: the commit that changed it unsigned, full hash. "" otherwise.
  commit: string
)

# A person's yes to the settings a SETTINGS_NOT_ALLOWED answer showed, for a repository of 'default'.
# outcome is ALLOWED, after which CanStart answers READY for them, or CHANGED when porelo.yml moved since
# the hash was answered: nothing is allowed, and CanStart shows the new list. A name that is no
# repository of 'default' is CHANGED too, with nothing to allow.
method AllowSettings(repository: string, hash: string) -> (outcome: string)

# Gives a task a changeable caption, or removes one.
#
# NOT a rename. A task's name is its identity in four places - the container, the gate ref it
# pushes to, the workspace and the log files - and moving a ref that may have unreviewed pushes
# behind it is a different and much larger thing. This changes what a person reads in a list and
# nothing else.
method Label(
  # Task name, as List reports it.
  task: string,
  # The caption. Omitted or blank removes it.
  label: ?string
) -> (
  #   LABELLED      the caption was written down
  #   CLEARED       the caption was removed
  #   NOT_A_TASK    no such task, or its state directory is gone
  #   NOT_RECORDED  it exists but recorded nothing about itself - started by an older Porelo
  #   FAILED        the profile could not be written
  outcome: string,
  # What the task's caption is now. "" after CLEARED, and "" for every outcome that changed
  # nothing.
  label: string
)

# Builds a project's task image without starting anything.
#
# The first task in a project otherwise waits minutes for something that has nothing to do with
# the work. Use with "more": a build takes minutes, and showing nothing for that long is
# indistinguishable from having hung.
method Prepare(
  # The name of a project, or the absolute path of its file as Projects reports it, which answers
  # for that name. An organization's project is read anew as a start reads it, with a start's
  # refusals in detail under FAILED. A path this machine does not report is NO_SUCH_PROJECT.
  project: string,
  # Whose tooling to install. Omit when only one agent is installed. An image with no agent is a
  # real thing to want - it is what somebody working in the container by hand needs - so this is
  # not refused when no agent is found.
  agent: ?string,
  # CACHED, AGENT or EVERYTHING. Default CACHED.
  #
  # CACHED is not "skip the build": the build runs and podman's cache decides layer by layer, so
  # an edited project file rebuilds what it changed and nothing else. It is what every task start
  # already does.
  #
  # AGENT replaces the agent's tooling and keeps the base image and its packages. There is no
  # podman flag for "rebuild from here" - it works by changing a build argument placed where the
  # agent's layers begin, which invalidates the cache from that line down. So it is real, and it
  # is a mechanism rather than a switch.
  #
  # EVERYTHING discards the base image's packages too, which means downloading them again.
  rebuild: ?string,
  # Says what it would do and does nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  # PREPARED, PREVIEWED, NO_SUCH_PROJECT or FAILED.
  outcome: string,
  # The image name, or "" when nothing was built.
  image: string,
  # The depth actually used, so an unrecognised one is visible rather than silently defaulted.
  rebuild: string,
  # Progress, when this was not called with "more". Empty when it was streamed.
  output: []string,
  # Why it failed, naming the step. "" otherwise.
  detail: string
)

# Which logs a task has.
#
# Ask rather than assume: what a task writes depends on what it started - a task with no gate
# has no gate.log, one run with --clearance off has no clearance.log - so a client holding a
# list of names would show an empty viewer for a file that was never going to exist, and would
# never show one a later release adds. Empty for a task whose state directory is gone, which is
# what removal does.
method Logs(task: string) -> (logs: []Log)

# Keeps a task's logs, and what Porelo says about it, in one archive on this machine.
#
# Written under $XDG_STATE_HOME/porelo/archives/, readable by the account only, as
# 'porelo task logs <task> --zip' writes it: the helpers' logs and events.jsonl, the task's
# status, files and talk log as text, and SHA256SUMS. A daemon has no current directory, so it is
# written there and 'path' says where; an interface offers to save it from there. What the agent
# itself printed (task.log) goes in only with 'withAgentOutput', since it may hold confidential data.
method LogArchive(task: string, withAgentOutput: ?bool) -> (path: string, files: []string)

# What a task holds that never reached the gate.
#
# Asked for ONE task, never on a listing. Every other field about a task comes from one 'ps' and
# the state directory; this one runs git inside the container, which on a list a client redraws
# would be a call per row - the cost 'since' and 'behindMeasured' both exist to avoid.
#
# 'readable' is not "holds nothing". Holding nothing is readable with two zeros. Unreadable means
# nobody could look: a task killed, or stopped by a Porelo that left no note. Absence rendering as
# nothing-to-worry-about is the failure this separation exists to prevent - the same one that put
# 'readable' on Credentials and Providers.
#
# 'asOf' is absent while the task runs, because the answer is current. For a stopped task it is
# when the answer was true: the workspace lives inside the container, so once it stops the only
# source is what the stop wrote down.
method WorkHeld(
  # Container name.
  task: string
) -> (
  # Whether what it holds could be established at all.
  readable: bool,
  # Files changed and not committed.
  changedFiles: int,
  # Commits on no remote.
  unpushedCommits: int,
  # When this was true, ISO-8601. Absent while the task runs.
  asOf: ?string
)

# One thing an image was built with, and where it came from: "project.yml", "porelo" for Porelo's own settings
# (all of the project default's that no checkout overrides, and the tools of every image), "agent package" for the
# agent, or the file a repository's own image was read from, as a start names it. Show an unknown one as it comes.
type ImageSetting (
  value: string,
  source: string
)

# What a task's image was built with, as the image recorded it at its build, whatever the settings say now.
#
# Answered for the image the task's container was made from, also after the project's image was built anew.
# A task whose image recorded nothing - built before Porelo recorded this - answers known false, with every
# value "" and every list empty: it does not know, and nothing is read from the project's file in its place. A
# label over 1 MiB answers the same. One limit stays: an image built before this, from a base image that itself
# carries this label, answers with the base image's claim, as nothing covered it.
# Empty lists with known true are an answer. A name that is no task here is NoSuchTask.
method ImageSettings(
  # Container name.
  task: string
) -> (
  # Whether the image recorded its settings.
  known: bool,
  baseImage: ImageSetting,
  # What the install commands (apt-get, apt, dnf, yum, microdnf, apk) in the build lines' RUN lines name, as
  # written, each with its line's source. Read from those lines, never run: an installer counts as a command's own
  # word, and a word after an option not known to stand alone is left out. What they fetch some other way is in
  # lines and not here.
  packages: []ImageSetting,
  # What Porelo asks for in every image, source "porelo". A base image may bring its own instead.
  tools: []ImageSetting,
  # The project's or repository's own build lines, each with its own source: an image's lines can come from
  # several places. As written, except that an ENV line (from image.env or a snippet) holds its names and
  # "<withheld>" for each value, and so does a URL's user and password (https://<withheld>@host). That catches
  # nothing else: a secret written into a snippet in any other way is shown as written. A record over 64 KiB loses
  # lines from the end, and its last line then says, with source "porelo", that it was cut.
  lines: []ImageSetting,
  # The agent whose tooling it carries. value "" for an image with none.
  agent: ImageSetting,
  # That agent's version; "" when its definition names none, or names anything that is not a version. From the
  # same source as agent.
  agentVersion: string,
  # offline, guarded or online.
  securityClass: ImageSetting,
  # When the image was built, ISO-8601. A later start that changed nothing reuses the image and keeps this.
  recorded: string,
  # What a repository's porelo.yml or the local override took away from the project's image. Empty for an
  # image that recorded none, and for every image built before the allowance of a repository's settings.
  removed: []RemovedSetting
)

method Tail(
  # Container name.
  task: string,
  # File name within the task's state directory, ending in .log. Not a path.
  log: string,
  # Start this many lines before the end instead of at the first line; then follow. A long log
  # is never sent whole for a client that shows its end.
  last: ?int,
  # Through the task's agent: each line as the agent shows it, the lines it hides left out. As
  # they are when the task has no agent here, or its agent formats nothing.
  formatted: ?bool
) -> (
  lines: []string,
  # Byte position in the log where this reply's first line starts, so a client can later ask for
  # what lies before it.
  offset: int
)

# What a task's terminal session shows, read without attaching: for work in a terminal, which has
# no log to follow. A snapshot, not a stream; a client asks again.
method Screen(
  # Container name.
  task: string,
  # How many of its last lines, at most the 10 000 a session keeps. 20 when absent.
  last: ?int,
  # Keep the colour and attribute escapes, for a client that draws them. Plain text when absent.
  escapes: ?bool
) -> (
  # The last lines of history and screen, a wrapped line joined into one, the blank screen below
  # them left out.
  lines: []string,
  # Whether the task has a terminal session: false for unattended work or a session that ended,
  # and then lines is empty.
  live: bool
)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- files handed to a task

# Hands a file to a running task. It appears in the container as /porelo/files/<name>, owned by root and
# readable by the agent, which cannot change or remove it, and it appears whole or not at all: nothing reaches
# the task until the last part has arrived and the content's sha256 is what the caller said.
#
# In parts, because a reply is one message: a whole request, the JSON around 'part' included, may be at most
# 4 MiB (4 194 304 bytes), so a part's base64 stays below about 4 000 000 characters. Any part from 1 byte up
# is accepted; parts of at most 2 MiB decoded are recommended, and a client on a slow link sends smaller ones.
# Every part repeats name, bytes and sha256, and 'offset' is where it starts. A client on another machine
# carries bytes over this, never a path, so the same call works from anywhere the socket reaches.
#
# A part whose offset is not what the daemon holds for that name, bytes and sha256 is refused as
# PartOutOfOrder, which says where to go on: a client resumes there after a lost connection. A hand-in nobody
# continues for 10 minutes is dropped, and the record says nothing of it - it never reached the task. A second
# hand-in of the same name with other bytes or sha256 while one is in flight is refused as HandInInProgress.
#
# A name already in /porelo/files is replaced, and the record says so. The task must be running. Nothing comes
# back out of a task through this or any other method here.
method HandIn(
  task: string,
  # A plain file name: not empty, at most 255 bytes, no '/', not starting with '.', no control characters.
  name: string,
  # The whole file's size.
  bytes: int,
  # The whole file's sha256, lower-case hex.
  sha256: string,
  # Where this part starts.
  offset: int,
  # The part, base64.
  part: string
) -> (
  # Bytes the daemon holds for this file.
  received: int,
  # Set once the last part made it complete and it is in the task.
  file: ?HandedFile
)

# Takes a handed-in file out of /porelo/files again. The task must be running.
method TakeBack(task: string, name: string) -> (file: HandedFile)

# Every hand-in, replacement and removal under a task's name, oldest first: of every run that carried the
# name, told apart by 'run' - and also after the task is gone, since the record is kept in the state
# directory. It names the files and never holds their content. It records what these methods did; a file
# copied into a container by other means is not in it.
method HandIns(task: string) -> (record: []HandIn)

# A file goes only to a running task, and only a running task gives one back.
error NoSuchStart(start: string)

error NotRunning(task: string)

# The file has more bytes than the task's handInLimit. Refused before any byte is kept.
error FileTooLarge(bytes: int, limit: int)

error FileNameRefused(name: string, reason: string)

# The parts did not add up to the sha256 that was said, or ran past 'bytes'. Nothing was placed.
error FileDiffers(expected: string, actual: string)

# The part does not start where the daemon is: go on at 'received'.
error PartOutOfOrder(name: string, received: int)

# Another file of that name is being handed in: 'received' of its 'bytes' are here.
error HandInInProgress(name: string, received: int, bytes: int)

# The task holds no handed-in file of that name.
error NoSuchFile(task: string, name: string)

# Start refuses a repository reached over ssh at a host this machine never met, before anything is
# made: CanStart answers the same as UNKNOWN_HOST_KEY. detail says what to do.
error UnknownHostKey(host: string, hostKeys: []HostKey, detail: string)

# Start refuses a repository whose upstream cannot be read when there is no mirror yet to start from,
# before anything is made: CanStart answers the same as UPSTREAM_<cause>. 'cause' is KEY_REFUSED,
# NOT_FOUND, UNREACHABLE or UNREADABLE, or UNKNOWN_HOST_KEY for a host whose key changed since it was
# trusted; 'fix' is the command that helps, or "".
error UpstreamUnreadable(cause: string, detail: string, fix: string)

# Start of a guarded or offline task whose name's earlier work still waits at the gate: nothing was created. 'commit'
# and 'subject' are that work's. Approve or reject it first, or start under another name.
error EarlierWorkWaits(task: string, commit: string, subject: string)

# Start of a task whose branch at the forge another task of the project's repository holds on this
# machine, in every class; or of an online task whose branch the upstream holds already, or whose
# project's branch.pattern matches the upstream's default branch, or when the upstream could not be
# asked: nothing was created. Passing work on would replace what the branch holds. 'task' is the task's
# name as Start settled it; 'detail' says which; name another branch with Start's 'branch' or other values.
error BranchTaken(task: string, branch: string, detail: string)

error NoSuchTask(task: string)

# The named log is not a file in that task's state directory.
error NoSuchLog(task: string, log: string)

# A gate method was called without naming a project file.
# A rebuild depth this daemon does not know.
#
# Refused rather than treated as the default: quietly doing the cheapest thing when somebody asked
# for the most expensive is exactly the failure the depths exist to prevent.
error UnknownRebuild(rebuild: string)
# Reported token totals; missing counts requests with an unreported value.
type UsageCount (
    tokens: ?int,
    missing: int
)

type UsageTotals (
    requests: int,
    input: UsageCount,
    cacheRead: UsageCount,
    cacheWrite: UsageCount,
    output: UsageCount,
    reasoning: UsageCount
)

type UsageGroup (
    name: string,
    totals: UsageTotals
)

# Local hours include the UTC offset so repeated hours remain separate.
type Usage (
    state: (available, absent, unreadable),
    totals: UsageTotals,
    models: []UsageGroup,
    agents: []UsageGroup,
    days: []UsageGroup,
    hours: []UsageGroup
)

# Reads current usage or the last retained record of a removed task, without network access.
method Usage(task: string) -> (usage: Usage)

# A package, a tool or another setting a first start found, and the file it was read from. Where
# something is set and could not be read without running something, value starts with "unknown: " and
# says why.
type Finding (
  what: string,
  value: string,
  source: string,
  note: string
)

# What a first start in a checkout on this computer is to do, changing no checkout or project. outcome is ASK_NEW
# (nothing is configured and something was recognized), ASK_UNKNOWN_FILE (a project.yml this machine has
# not approved), KNOWN (nothing to ask), NOT_DEFAULT (a repository of an organization's project),
# NO_PLUGIN or NOTHING_RECOGNIZED. A new draft is porelo.yml and project is 'default'; an existing
# porelo.yml is KNOWN, with its settings still checked by CanStart. An existing local project keeps its
# project.yml approval and the files it names. found, hosts and marked are for a person to read before a
# yes, and 'marked' names the hosts only the checkout names, in no curated set,
# for which a yes is not the default. file is the text that would be written; for a project.yml already
# there, every line of it and of each file it names, under a line '--- <name> ---', made printable. hash is
# what the answer names: exactly the draft's text and filename, or an existing project file and each
# file it names, with their names. An interface then starts as usual, by the project's name.
# refusal says why a project.yml here cannot be taken, for a person to read as a warning, and "" otherwise: the outcome is then
# ASK_UNKNOWN_FILE with no file and no hash, and the start goes on in 'default' without it.
# notes are for a person to read above the question, for any outcome: an adapter that failed or was refused, what an
# adapter read and could place nowhere, and a lookup that failed. An approved local project's checkout
# gets one line of advice about porelo.yml, once per checkout on this machine, remembered in state.
# A note is not part of what a hash names.
# Where a file would be made, each adapter first looks up what it read (Resolve), the one time an adapter opens a
# connection: the lines it fetched are in found, each 'image line' whole, and in the file and its hash. proxy holds the
# caller's HTTP_PROXY, HTTPS_PROXY, NO_PROXY and their lower-case forms, as its environment has them; only these names are
# read, and only an adapter's process gets them. They never appear in an answer, a note or a log. Without proxy, an
# adapter looks up with none.
# fileName names the file a yes writes or approves: "porelo.yml" for a new repository draft in 'default',
# "project.yml" for an existing local project, or "" when no file is named.
method FirstStart(checkout: string, proxy: ?[string]string) -> (outcome: string, found: []Finding, hosts: []string, marked: []string, file: string, fileName: string, project: string, hash: string, notes: []string, refusal: string)

# A person's yes to the text a hash names: writes a new porelo.yml into the checkout, adds the checkout to
# 'default' and allows what its repository settings ask, so the following start asks nothing again.
# For an existing project.yml it makes this machine's copy the project, which a start by the project's
# name reads whatever the checkout then holds. A
# No has no call, and nothing is kept. The lookup FirstStart made answers the yes; one no longer kept is made
# anew, through proxy as FirstStart takes it, and a text that changed with it is MOVED_ON.
method ConfirmFirstStart(checkout: string, hash: string, proxy: ?[string]string) -> (project: string)

# A first start's yes cannot be taken. reason is NOT_A_CHECKOUT, NAME_TAKEN (the name is 'default', another
# local project's, or a project approved for another host, port or path), NO_NAME, FILE_UNREADABLE, MOVED_ON (the text is no longer what the hash named),
# NOTHING_ASKED or NOT_WRITTEN; detail says it in words.
error FirstStartRefused(reason: string, detail: string)

# A project this machine knows about.
#
# Not something Porelo creates: a project is somebody's directory with a project.yml in it, and
# this reports what has been seen of one rather than owning it.
type Project (
  # Project name, as its file declares it.
  name: string,
  # offline, guarded or online. "" when no task has recorded one.
  securityClass: string,
  # Absolute path of its project.yml on the daemon's machine, which every gate method and Start
  # take. "" when nothing recorded one, or when the recorded file is no longer there - reported
  # absent rather than as a path nothing can read, so a call made with it cannot fail for a
  # reason that looks like a fault in the daemon.
  file: string,
  # A host carries it for this person and it has no file here yet, so file is "". Nothing fetched
  # it to say so. TakeProject brings the file, when the person acts on this row; false everywhere
  # else, and once the file is here.
  carried: bool,
  # The gate's mirror for it. "" when it has never used the gate.
  mirror: string,
  # How many pushes are waiting for review in that mirror.
  pending: int,
  # How many of its tasks exist right now, running or stopped.
  tasks: int,
  # How many of those are up. A project with none is the ordinary case - between tasks, or after
  # one was stopped and can still be resumed - so both numbers are given rather than one being
  # inferred from the other. This list is every project, not only the busy ones.
  running: int,
  # Whether a task image for this project exists, so work can start here without building one
  # first. That is the whole claim: it does NOT say the image still matches the project file as
  # it stands now. A project that has never run answers false, which is a normal state and not a
  # fault - it means the first task here will spend minutes building before anything happens.
  prepared: bool,
  # Latest recorded task start or message event of this project on this machine, ISO-8601 instant.
  # "" when none can be read. Not a judgment that the project is unused.
  lastUsed: string,
  # The repositories this project has, its own first.
  #
  # Never empty for a project whose file can be read: every project has its own repository. Empty
  # means the file could not be read, which is NOT the same as a project with one repository - do
  # not render it as one.
  #
  # Objects rather than parallel lists of names, mirrors and counts: lists indexed together drift
  # the moment one is built from a different source, and the drift is silent.
  repositories: []Repository,
  # ABSENT, READY, STALE or UNKNOWN - what 'prepared' cannot say.
  #
  # STALE means an image exists and was built before the project file changed under it, so work
  # would start in something the file no longer describes and nothing would mention it. That is
  # the state this field exists for; a bool could only ever say whether something was there.
  #
  # UNKNOWN is an image that does not record what it was built from - built by an older Porelo, or
  # belonging to a project whose file has moved. Not stale: nothing here knows either way, and
  # reporting it as stale would send somebody rebuilding for no reason.
  #
  # Only the base image and the image snippet decide this. Egress, limits and the upstream change
  # what a task may do rather than what it is built from, and counting them would call an image
  # stale after an edit that could not have changed it - which teaches people to ignore the word.
  preparedState: string,
  # Commits the upstream has that this project's mirror does not. MEANINGLESS unless
  # behindReason is MEASURED - it is 0 for every other reason, and 0 there does not mean up to
  # date. Never render this number without behindMeasured beside it.
  behind: int,
  # When 'behind' was measured, RFC 3339. "" when it never was.
  #
  # Not optional decoration. The daemon measures on a timer, because a reply field that fetched
  # would make this list occasionally take thirty seconds for reasons nothing on screen explains
  # - so what you get is a number with an age, and the only thing worse than a stale answer is a
  # stale answer that looks fresh.
  behindMeasured: string,
  # Why 'behind' is what it is. New values may appear; render an unrecognised one rather than
  # throwing, by the same rule as every other enumeration here.
  #
  #   MEASURED       the number is real, and behindMeasured says when it was taken
  #   NEVER_CHECKED  nothing has looked yet. NOT the same as being up to date
  #   NO_UPSTREAM    the mirror was created empty, so there is nothing to be behind
  #   OFFLINE        the project reaches nothing by class, so asking would break that promise
  #   FAILED         looking failed; behindDetail says what git said
  behindReason: string,
  # What went wrong, for FAILED. "" otherwise. Prose for a person - never parsed, and never
  # rendered as a number.
  behindDetail: string,
  # What project.yml names under 'credentials:': a vault entry's name to the destination it is for, which
  # every task of the project holds. Names only, never a value.
  credentials: [string]string,
  # The project's conversation - its room - when a peer is reached through a transport's own ('<scheme>:');
  # absent otherwise.
  messages: ?ProjectMessages
)

# What a host offers, so a person can compare it with what they were told.
#
# Shown, never recorded: recording is TrustHostKey, which takes the fingerprint back. Accepting a
# host key on first use would be easier and is exactly what an interception looks like, so this
# machine refuses to decide it - attended or not, because a timer that learns a host at three in
# the morning has answered the question this refuses to answer.
method HostKeys(
  # The host on its port, as a person types it: 'forge.example.org', and off port 22
  # 'forge.example.org:7999' or '[2001:db8::1]:7999' - ssh remembers a host on another port
  # as another host. A port of 22 written out is the same as none. Answered in that form.
  host: string
) -> (
  host: string,
  # Whether this machine already remembers a key for it.
  known: bool,
  keys: []HostKey
)

# Records ONE key for a host: the one a person confirmed.
#
# The fingerprint comes from them, not from here. The host is asked again and the key is recorded
# only if it still offers that one - which is what stops a key that arrived between being shown
# and being confirmed from being the one written down.
method TrustHostKey(
  # As HostKeys takes it: with its port off port 22.
  host: string,
  # SHA256:..., exactly as HostKeys gave it.
  fingerprint: string
) -> (
  recorded: bool,
  type: string,
  fingerprint: string,
  detail: string
)

# Every project this machine knows about, alphabetically.
#
# The list is assembled from what is durable: the gate mirrors, the tasks that exist, and the
# project files task starts have recorded. A project appears here once somebody has run a task
# with it - there is no registration step, because nobody would run one.
#
# This is the method that makes the gate reachable from an interface. Pending, Review, Approve,
# Reject and Start all take a project file path, and a client on another machine has no
# filesystem here to find one in.
# One thing a project deletion removes.
type Removal (
  # MIRROR, IMAGE, BUILD, REGISTRY, UPSTREAM_RECORD or TASK.
  kind: string,
  # The thing itself - a path, an image name, a container name - as a person would recognise it.
  what: string
)

method Projects() -> (projects: []Project)

# Brings the file of a project Project.carried names, as a start would. For a person's act, never
# for a list: it fetches once, and on a host replaces the person's forge token.
#
# A refusal is an answer: file is "", reason is the contract's word, and words say it to a person.
# On success reason and words are "". A name this machine does not carry for this person is
# reason "not-carried" and writes nothing.
method TakeProject(project: string) -> (file: string, reason: string, words: string)

# The same answer, again whenever it changes.
#
# Called without "more" this answers once, exactly as Projects does, so a client that cannot
# stream is not locked out of the data.
#
# Called with "more" it sends the whole list on every change and nothing in between. Unlike
# Watch there is no age to exclude: every field here is a count, a state or a recorded
# moment, and "behindMeasured" moving IS a change - the age an interface draws beside the
# number resets with it.
#
# Slower than Watch on purpose. A scan runs the container runtime and reads the gate's refs
# for every project, and what it answers changes on human timescales: a project created, a
# push arriving at the gate, an environment prepared. The one place where latency costs
# something real is Prompts, where a question expires; work waiting at the gate has no
# deadline.
method WatchProjects() -> (projects: []Project)

# Measures one repository's distance from its upstream, now.
#
# Its own operation on purpose. The distance is otherwise measured on a timer and read from a
# record, because measuring reaches the network - and a listing that fetched would make the queue
# cost what a listing must not. So this cannot be a flag on a read; it is something somebody asked
# for.
#
# It goes through the same measurement the timer uses and writes the same record, so a triggered
# fetch and a timed one cannot disagree about how far behind a project is, and Projects shows
# whichever ran last without being told.
method SyncUpstream(
  # Project NAME, as Projects.name gives it.
  project: string,
  # Which of the project's repositories to measure. Default: the project's own.
  #
  # Each has its own mirror and its own upstream, so a project's distance was never one number
  # once it had a second repository. Measuring one and showing it against all of them would be a
  # number that is right about one repository.
  repository: ?string) -> (
  # MEASURED, NO_SUCH_PROJECT, NO_MIRROR, UNREADABLE or FAILED.
  #
  # NO_SUCH_PROJECT also answers a repository the project does not have; 'detail' says which.
  #
  # NO_MIRROR is ordinary: a project that has never used the gate has nothing to measure against.
  outcome: string,
  # Commits the upstream has that this repository's mirror does not. MEANINGLESS unless
  # 'measured'.
  behind: int,
  # Whether the number above means anything. False for a repository with no upstream, and for one
  # this machine could not reach.
  measured: bool,
  # What the measurement itself reported: MEASURED, NEVER_CHECKED, NO_UPSTREAM, OFFLINE or FAILED.
  reason: string,
  detail: string
)

# What backups have been taken of a project's mirror.
#
# A bundle is written wherever an operator names it, so nothing could work this out afterwards -
# this is read from a record written when each one was taken. An interface cannot offer to restore
# from a list it cannot read, which is what this is for.
#
# Newest first. Empty is an ordinary answer: a project nobody has backed up, or one backed up by a
# Porelo older than the record.
method Backups(
  # Project NAME, as Projects.name gives it.
  project: string,
  # Which of the project's repositories the backups are of. Default: the project's own.
  #
  # A bundle belongs to ONE repository's mirror. Asking without this and restoring what comes back
  # into a different repository would write one history over another's.
  repository: ?string) -> (backups: []Backup)

type Backup (
  # When it was written, ISO-8601.
  taken: string,
  # Absolute path on the daemon's machine.
  bundle: string,
  # How many pushes were waiting for review when it was taken. What a restore would bring back.
  refs: int,
  # Whether that file is still there. A record is not the bundle: the file can be moved, deleted
  # or replaced afterwards and nothing here would know, so an absent one is shown rather than
  # quietly dropped - somebody moved it, and that is worth seeing.
  present: bool,
  # Its size now, or 0 when it is gone. Not what it was when taken: this is read from disk, and
  # claiming a size for a file that is not there would be inventing one.
  bytes: int
)

# Restores a project's mirror from a bundle.
#
# Refuses when the mirror holds pushes nobody has reviewed, and names them. Those exist ONLY in the
# mirror - not on the upstream, not in a workspace, not in the bundle being restored - so
# overwriting one destroys the only copy. Same outcome name as DeleteProject and Stop, for the same
# situation.
method RestoreBackup(
  # Project NAME, as Projects.name gives it.
  project: string,
  # Which of the project's repositories to restore INTO. Default: the project's own.
  #
  # This writes over a mirror, and the pushes it destroys exist only there - so a bundle taken of
  # one repository restored into another destroys the only copy of work that was never its. Pass
  # the repository the bundle came from, as Backups was asked for it.
  repository: ?string,
  # Absolute path of the bundle, as Backups reports it.
  bundle: string,
  # Says what would happen and changes nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool,
  # Proceeds although unreviewed work would be destroyed.
  force: ?bool
) -> (
  # RESTORED, PREVIEWED, HOLDS_WORK, NO_SUCH_PROJECT, NO_SUCH_BACKUP or FAILED.
  outcome: string,
  # The mirror that would be written, or was.
  mirror: string,
  # Refs nobody reviewed. Non-empty with HOLDS_WORK, and also under force - it is what force
  # destroys, so it is worth showing afterwards as well as before.
  unreviewed: []string,
  detail: string
)

# Removes one backup: the bundle file and the record of it.
#
# The bundle may be somebody's only copy of work that never reached the gate, so this takes the
# path rather than an index - a list that shifted between reading it and acting on it would
# otherwise delete a different backup than the one somebody chose.
method DeleteBackup(
  # Project NAME, as Projects.name gives it.
  project: string,
  # Absolute path, exactly as Backups reported it.
  bundle: string,
  # Reports what would go, having removed nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  # DELETED, PREVIEWED, NO_SUCH_BACKUP or FAILED.
  outcome: string,
  # Whether the file itself was there to remove. False with DELETED means the record was cleared
  # for a bundle somebody had already moved - which is a tidy-up, not a loss.
  fileRemoved: bool,
  # How many pushes it held, from the record. What a person is giving up.
  refs: int,
  detail: string
)

# The repositories of the project 'default': where work goes on a repository that no other project
# names. Every machine has 'default', its settings are Porelo's
# ('guarded', nothing reachable beyond the agent's provider, blocked connections refused, no
# conversation), and it is never removed. A task is started in it with Start(project: "default",
# repository: <name>) like any other. Removing it, giving its
# tasks egress or a credential, and choosing another upstream are refused, naming a project
# repository as the way.
method DefaultRepositories() -> (repositories: []DefaultRepository)

# Adds a repository to 'default' by its address - named by hand or picked from a forge's list. The
# same address again answers the entry already there. Refused with Failed for a name that is taken
# or is not a repository name.
method AddToDefault(
  # Where its approved work goes: an ssh or https address, or a path on this machine.
  upstream: string,
  # What to call it. Default: after its address.
  name: ?string
) -> (repository: DefaultRepository)

# Takes a repository out of 'default'. Its mirror, and anything waiting in it, stays. 'removed' is
# false when 'default' has no repository of that name. This machine's deploy key for it is forgotten
# and answered in 'keys', as RemoveProject answers them, so it is removed at the forge too.
method RemoveFromDefault(
  # The repository's name in 'default'.
  name: string
) -> (removed: bool, keys: []MachineDeployKey)

# One repository of 'default'.
type DefaultRepository (
  # What Start's 'repository' takes.
  name: string,
  # Where its approved work goes.
  upstream: string,
  # The checkout it was added from on this machine, or "" when it was added by address.
  checkout: string,
  # Where its history comes from and its approved work goes, one place both ways: CHECKOUT (the checkout it was
  # started in, whose remote the person pulls and pushes themselves; upstream is then that checkout's path) or REMOTE
  # (an address; no checkout is part of it).
  source: string,
  # The checkout's origin when it was added from one, shown only and never reached; else the address.
  remote: string,
  # The placed project that names the same repository, or "". Its tasks then start there, and an
  # interface offers to take the repository out of 'default'.
  claimedBy: string
)

# Shows what nothing on this machine owns any more, and removes it with apply - what 'porelo prune'
# does. Orphaned means only: a project with no running task and no readable project
# file, with its mirrors, image, build context and stopped tasks; what is kept for a task whose
# container is gone; and images, build contexts, records and vault entries named after a project
# that exists nowhere. A running task and a placed project are never in it.
#
# What holds work nobody has looked at - a push nobody reviewed, a stopped task's workspace, a
# message waiting for a person - is in 'keeps', saying what in 'holds', and stays unless
# includingWork. Without apply nothing is removed and 'removes' is what would go.
method Prune(
  # Remove what is found. Without it nothing is removed.
  apply: ?bool,
  # Remove what holds work nobody has looked at too.
  includingWork: ?bool
) -> (
  applied: bool,
  removes: []PrunedThing,
  keeps: []PrunedThing,
  # What could not be looked at, and why: a locked vault, for one.
  unchecked: []string
)

# One thing Prune found.
type PrunedThing (
  # PROJECT, TASK, IMAGE, BUILD, RECORD or VAULT.
  kind: string,
  # A project, a container, an image, a path or a vault entry.
  name: string,
  # What of it goes, as a person recognises it.
  what: []string,
  # What it holds that nobody has looked at, or why it was not removed; "" for what goes.
  holds: string
)

# One repository of a project, and the state that belongs to it rather than to the project.
#
# A project is a unit of work over one or more repositories and a task works on exactly one of
# them, so each of these has its own mirror, its own review branch and its own distance from its
# own upstream. A screen that showed one of each per PROJECT would be showing one repository's
# numbers against all of them.
type Repository (
  # What Start and the gate methods take as 'repository'.
  name: string,
  # Whether this is the project's own repository - where project.yml, the planning and the issues
  # live. Reported rather than left to be worked out by comparing this to the project's name.
  own: bool,
  # Where approved work goes, or "" when it has none. A repository without an upstream is one
  # whose work stays on this machine, which is a legitimate state.
  upstream: string,
  # The bare mirror on this machine, or "" when it has never been used.
  mirror: string,
  # Pushes waiting for review in that mirror. The project's own 'pending' is the sum of these.
  pending: int,
  # Commits this repository's upstream has that its mirror does not. MEANINGLESS unless
  # behindReason is MEASURED, exactly as on the project. Never render it without behindMeasured.
  behind: int,
  # When 'behind' was measured, RFC 3339. "" when it never was.
  behindMeasured: string,
  # Why 'behind' is what it is. Same values as the project's.
  behindReason: string,
  behindDetail: string,
  # Everything a task on this repository may reach, each saying which block granted it: always the
  # project's, since what a task may reach is the project's alone.
  egress: []Grant,
  # What a task on this repository may consume, and where each key came from: always the project.
  limits: ResolvedLimits,
  # The agent the project file names for this repository, by the name Agents reports; "" when it
  # names none. Start takes it when its own 'agent' is omitted. Whether it is installed here is
  # CanStart's question.
  agent: string,
  # The nickname the project file gives this repository's agent, or "" when it gives none.
  nickname: string,
  # The name this repository's agent goes by in a conversation and on its commits: "Agent
  # <nickname>", or "Agent <Name>", capitalized, without one. Show it as it is; never build it.
  goesBy: string
)

# One thing a repository may reach.
type Grant (
  # The set name or the host.
  value: string,
  # "set" or "domain".
  kind: string,
  # Which block declared it: "project", since what a task may reach is the project's alone.
  from: string
)

# What a task on one repository may consume, resolved.
#
# What a task may consume is the project's alone, so the three '...From' fields say "project" - which
# covers BOTH a value the project file wrote and Porelo's own default, which this side cannot tell
# apart today. Do not render "project" as "written by hand".
type ResolvedLimits (
  # Memory limit, or "" for no limit.
  memory: string,
  # CPU limit, or "" for no limit.
  cpus: string,
  # Process limit.
  pids: int,
  memoryFrom: string,
  cpusFrom: string,
  pidsFrom: string
)

# Removes a project of this machine alone: a file a person wrote, and what Porelo built for it.
#
# It deletes, so it refuses first: a mirror may hold pushes nobody has reviewed and tasks may still
# be up. A project of the organization and 'default' are refused with NotAllowed, and a name this
# machine does not list with NoSuchProject.
method RemoveProject(
  name: string,
  # Says what would go, and removes nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool,
  # Removes it although work is waiting for review. That work is destroyed.
  force: ?bool
) -> (
  # DELETED, PREVIEWED, HOLDS_WORK, TASKS_RUNNING or FAILED.
  outcome: string,
  # Refs nobody reviewed, with HOLDS_WORK and with force - it is what force destroys.
  unreviewed: []string,
  # Tasks that are up, with TASKS_RUNNING.
  running: []string,
  # What goes: the mirrors, the task image, the build directory, the registry entry, the recorded
  # distance, the project's file and every task. Filled for a REFUSAL too, so the cost stands
  # beside the reason.
  removes: []Removal,
  # What is deliberately NOT touched - the real upstream above all. Named so a confirmation never
  # lists the operator's own things among the casualties.
  keeps: []string,
  detail: string,
  # This machine's deploy keys for the project, which it forgot with it: remove each at the forge by its
  # title and fingerprint - that is what grants access. Empty with a refusal and a preview.
  keys: []MachineDeployKey,
  # This machine's message key, whose line the project's machine-signers should lose.
  signer: MachineKey
)

# Clears a project from this machine, or with no project the whole account, in one step - 'porelo project
# clear' and 'porelo clear', from the same code.
#
# Per project: its tasks with their workspaces and mailboxes (refused originals included), its mirrors and
# image, its conversation through the transport's 'clear' and what this machine kept for it,
# and its deploy keys from the vault. The account: every project, the built-in project's tasks, then what
# each transport keeps for the account. The vault, the person's keys and the packages stay.
#
# What only a person's credentials can remove is answered, never attempted: the deploy keys at the forges
# (keys) and this machine's line in each project's machine-signers (signer). Run twice, the second answer
# has no items.
method Clear(
  # The project, or empty for the whole account.
  project: ?string,
  # Says what would go, and removes nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool,
  # Clears work nobody reviewed and running tasks too. That work is destroyed.
  force: ?bool
) -> (
  # Each thing, in the order it was handled.
  items: []ClearedItem,
  # The deploy keys this machine had at forges, for a person to remove there by title and fingerprint.
  keys: []MachineDeployKey,
  # This machine's machine-signers line, for a person to take out of each project that lists it; "" when
  # it has no message key.
  signer: string,
  # Whether it stopped before removing anything: work nobody reviewed, or a running task. force clears it.
  refused: bool
)

# This machine's message key, as a project's machine-signers names it.
type MachineKey (
  # 'porelo@<host>'.
  principal: string,
  # 'ssh-ed25519 AAAA...'.
  key: string,
  # The machine-signers line: principal and key.
  line: string,
  fingerprint: string
)

# Retired: always answers DeployKeyRefused. Git uses the person's own ssh key for an ssh address,
# or their token stored as git.token.<host> for an https address.
method DeployKey(
  project: string,
  # Default: the project's own.
  repository: ?string,
  # Retained for compatibility; ignored.
  readOnly: ?bool,
  # Retained for compatibility; ignored.
  new: ?bool,
  # The repository's address, used to name its host when the project is unknown.
  upstream: ?string
) -> (key: MachineDeployKey)

# The keys a project file may hold in each section, as this machine's Porelo knows them - for an editor
# built from what the machine would accept. 'sections' maps a section ("" is the top level;
# 'mail.peers.<peer>' and 'repositories.<repository>' stand for each named one) to its keys; 'open' names
# what Porelo does not check - the project's own names and each transport's settings. 'keys' describes
# every key in 'sections', so a form a person new to Porelo can fill is built from Porelo's own words.
method ProjectFileSchema() -> (sections: [string][]string, open: []string, keys: []ProjectFileKey)

# One key a project file may hold, described.
type ProjectFileKey (
  # Where it sits, as in 'sections': "" for the top level, 'mail.peers.<peer>' and
  # 'repositories.<repository>' for each named one.
  section: string,
  name: string,
  # What it means, in one sentence.
  describe: string,
  # string, boolean, integer, list or map.
  type: string,
  # The values it takes when they are a fixed set, such as security_class's; empty otherwise.
  values: []string,
  # What it is when the file does not say, spelt as the file would; "" for nothing.
  default: string,
  # Whether a project file without it is refused.
  required: bool
)

# Checks a draft project.yml against this machine, before a person commits it. 'refused' is what Porelo
# refuses anywhere and blocks the commit; 'refusedHere' is what this machine's follow would refuse (an egress
# set it does not have); 'warnings' is what only this machine lacks, or a key a later Porelo may know.
#
# A section under 'mail.transports' is asked of that transport here, when it is installed and its describe
# lists 'settings': what it refuses anywhere comes back in 'refused', what it warns of in 'warnings', each
# beginning 'mail.transports.<scheme>: '. A transport that does not offer the check leaves its section open.
method CheckProjectFile(text: string) -> (refused: []string, refusedHere: []string, warnings: []string)

# The deploy key could not be had: no upstream a key can be for, or the vault is locked.
error DeployKeyRefused(message: string)

# This machine lists no project of that name.
#
# An error rather than an empty answer, for the same reason NoSuchTask is one: a caller that asked
# about something that is not there has a wrong argument, and reporting it as "nothing to do" lets
# that wrong argument arrive as a legitimate result.
# The acting person may not do this; 'message' says why.
error NotAllowed(message: string)

error NoSuchProject(project: string)

# A curated group of hosts a project can opt into by name.
type EgressSet (
  # What a project writes in its egress.sets list, and what SetEgress takes.
  name: string,
  # Human-readable name, for a listing.
  label: string,
  # The hosts it grants, in the order the file declares them.
  domains: []string
)

# A host a task may reach, and who granted it.
type EgressHost (
  host: string,
  # "agent <name>", "provider <name>", "set <name>", "project", "upstream", or, with a repository's own settings's
  # step (2), "porelo.yml of <repository>" and "the local override". The first grant wins, so a host
  # in both a set and an agent's list is reported as the agent's.
  origin: string
)

# How far a change to a running task goes. There is no default: "this run only" and "this run
# and the project file" are different intentions, and a client that did not say which it meant
# must not have one picked for it.
type Scope (
  # The running task, and nothing else. The project file is untouched and the next task will
  # ask again - a resumed task loses it too, because its ruleset and resolver are rebuilt from
  # what is on disk.
  RUN,
  # The running task, and detail says what a person writes into the project file so the next task
  # starts with it. Porelo changes no project file itself.
  RUN_AND_PROJECT
)

# What became of widening a running task.
type WidenOutcome (
  # Applied to the running task. With RUN_AND_PROJECT, detail names the project file and holds the
  # egress section a person writes into it; persisted is always false.
  WIDENED,
  # NarrowTask: taken back from the running task.
  NARROWED,
  # What it would do, having changed nothing.
  PREVIEWED,
  # Every name was already granted to this run.
  NO_CHANGE,
  # No such task, or it is not up. Nothing can be changed in a container that is gone.
  NOT_RUNNING,
  # The project is offline, so its tasks reach nothing and this is not a way around that.
  REFUSED_BY_CLASS,
  # A name asked for is refused for this task - by its agent, its project or its repository - and
  # a widening does not take a refusal back. Nothing was changed; detail names it and who refused.
  REFUSED_NAME,
  # The run WAS changed, and no section for the project file can be said: nothing knows where it
  # is, or the change would not read there. Not a failure: the task can now reach the host, and
  # detail says why.
  NO_PROJECT_FILE,
  # The run could not be widened at all. detail says why.
  FAILED
)

# What became of a change to a project's egress.
type EgressOutcome (
  # No longer sent: Porelo changes no project file. A change without dryRun is refused with Failed.
  CHANGED,
  # What it would do, with dryRun set; entry is the egress section to write.
  PREVIEWED,
  # The file already says that.
  NO_CHANGE,
  # A named set is not installed on this machine, so the file would name nothing real. Nothing
  # was written; detail says which.
  NO_SUCH_SET,
  # The project's security class forbids declaring egress at all - an offline project declares
  # nothing. detail says so in the words the project reader uses.
  REFUSED_BY_CLASS,
  # The project file could not be read. detail says why.
  UNREADABLE,
  # No longer sent: Porelo changes no project file.
  NOT_WRITTEN
)

# Reads one of a task's log files, and with "more" follows it as it is written.
#
# The log name is checked against the files that are there rather than resolved as a path.
# The curated egress sets installed on this machine.
#
# What a chooser offers, so nobody has to author a host list. Scanned from a directory each time
# it is asked, so a set an operator dropped in appears without anything being rebuilt - and
# theirs wins over a packaged one of the same name, which is why the locations are given in the
# order they are searched.
#
# Not every set can be complete: os-packages-fedora reaches mirrors named by a mirrorlist, which
# differ by region and by day, so hosts beyond the list arrive as clearance prompts. An interface
# should not present it as equivalent to the others.
method Sets() -> (
  sets: []EgressSet,
  # Directories searched, most specific first. Empty means none is installed.
  locations: []string
)

# What a project may reach, and who granted each host.
#
# The agent's own hosts and its provider's are included, because what an operator wants to know
# is what a task will reach and half of it is not in the project file. Which agent that is
# follows the same rule a run does: the one named, the repository's, or the only one installed.
method Egress(
  # Project NAME, as Projects.name gives it.
  project: string,
  # Which of its repositories to answer for. Default: the project's own.
  #
  # What a task may reach is the project's alone, so every repository answers the same; one the
  # file does not name is refused. Each host says what granted it, in EgressHost.origin.
  repository: ?string,
  # Agent whose grants to include. Default: the agent the project file names for the repository,
  # else the only one installed.
  agent: ?string
) -> (
  hosts: []EgressHost,
  # Hosts an agent declares it asks for and is deliberately not given. Distinguishable from a
  # host nobody added, which is the distinction the firewall itself cannot make.
  refused: []string,
  # What the repository's porelo.yml or the person's .porelo.yml beside it took away, in 'default', each
  # with the file that did: from the project's egress or from what the checked-in file added. Empty
  # outside 'default' and without a repository.
  removed: []RemovedSetting
)

# Says what a change to what a project may reach would do, and the egress section a person writes
# for it.
#
# The highest-consequence change in the product, so it answers in hosts rather than in set names: a
# set is a name for several hosts and whoever adds one is entitled to see them. Porelo changes no
# project file: with dryRun the answer is PREVIEWED and entry holds the egress section as the file
# must read, checked in the whole file by the reader that would read it later. Without dryRun a
# change is refused with Failed, whose message names the file and holds the same section.
#
# Nothing here applies to a task that is already running: a container's ruleset and resolver are
# built when it starts.
method SetEgress(
  # Project NAME, as Projects.name gives it.
  project: string,
  # Which of the project's repositories the change is asked for. Default: the project's own.
  #
  # What a task may reach is the project's alone, so the section is the project's egress whichever
  # repository is named; one the file does not name is refused.
  repository: ?string,
  # Curated sets to declare. A set this machine does not have is refused.
  addSets: ?[]string,
  removeSets: ?[]string,
  # Hosts to declare directly, for a mirror no set covers.
  addDomains: ?[]string,
  removeDomains: ?[]string,
  # Answer PREVIEWED with entry rather than refusing; nothing is written either way.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  outcome: EgressOutcome,
  # Hosts the change adds, with the origin that grants each. In the order they were granted.
  opens: []EgressHost,
  # Hosts it takes away.
  closes: []EgressHost,
  # What reaching a forge costs a guarded project, when this change opens one and the project
  # did not already reach one. "" otherwise - said once, when it becomes true.
  cost: string,
  # Why it was refused. "" when nothing went wrong.
  detail: string,
  # With PREVIEWED, the egress section as the project file must read for the change, YAML in the
  # file's own form, which a person writes. Absent otherwise.
  entry: ?string
)

# Gives a task that is already running somewhere new to reach.
#
# The alternative is to stop the run and start another one, which throws away a workspace, a gate
# token, commits that never reached the gate and an agent halfway through something - to add one
# host. This changes it in place: the resolver is told without being restarted, and the name is
# recorded so the clearance watcher lets it through without asking anybody.
#
# Names only. A set is a name for several hosts and granting one would be the same operation
# repeated; narrowing a running task is not offered at all, because taking a grant away from a
# container that already has it is a different thing and is not decided yet.
#
# **The attempt that was already refused is not retried.** The packet was dropped; whether the
# agent tries again is the agent's business. An interface may say the host is now reachable and
# must not say that the request which just failed will now succeed.
# Takes names back from a running task, and optionally says what to write into its project file.
#
# THIS STOPS NEW CONNECTIONS AND NOT THE ONES ALREADY RUNNING. The name stops resolving and its
# recorded addresses come out of the firewall, so nothing new can be reached - but the ruleset
# accepts established traffic without consulting the set again, so a transfer in progress runs to
# its end. Do not render this as "the host is now unreachable": it is not, yet. If a transfer has
# to stop, stopping the task is what does that.
#
# The addresses removed are the ones recorded when each grant was applied, never the answer to
# resolving the name again now - a CDN or round-robin answers the daemon and the container
# differently, and the addresses that differ are exactly the ones that would be left open.
#
# 'scope' is required, exactly as for WidenTask. Narrowing only the run when somebody meant the
# project too leaves the next task starting with the host still open.
# Turns enforcement on or off on a task that is already running.
#
# Deliberately not WidenTask with a special value. Those grant and withdraw names, and that the
# firewall stays loaded is what they mean; folding "stop asking about anything" into them would
# make one method mean two unrelated things, and the quiet one would be the dangerous one.
#
# What it cannot undo: turning enforcement off does not recall a connection that was already
# refused - the packet was dropped and nothing retries it - and turning it back on does not recall
# anything waved through while it was off. The ruleset is loaded throughout, so this never opens a
# destination by itself; what changes is whether a blocked connection produces a question.
method SetClearance(
  task: string,
  # prompt, allow, deny or off.
  mode: string,
  # Says what would happen and changes nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  # CHANGED, UNCHANGED, PREVIEWED, NO_SUCH_TASK, NOT_RUNNING, UNKNOWN_MODE, NOT_RECORDED or
  # FAILED.
  #
  # UNCHANGED is not a failure: it was already in that mode and nothing was restarted, which is
  # worth saying rather than reporting a change that did not happen.
  outcome: string,
  # The mode before. "" for a task started before this was recorded.
  was: string,
  # The mode after. "" when nothing changed.
  now: string,
  detail: string
)

method NarrowTask(
  # Task name, as List reports it.
  task: string,
  # Names to take back. Anything not currently granted to this run is ignored, not an error.
  domains: []string,
  # RUN or RUN_AND_PROJECT. Required; there is no default.
  scope: Scope,
  # Reports what it would take back, having changed nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  #   NARROWED         taken back from the run; with RUN_AND_PROJECT detail says what to write
  #   PREVIEWED        what it would take back, having changed nothing
  #   NO_CHANGE        none of these names is granted to this run
  #   NOT_RUNNING      no such task, or it is not up
  #   NO_PROJECT_FILE  the RUN was narrowed and no section can be said; NOT a failure
  #   FAILED           the run could not be narrowed; detail says why
  outcome: WidenOutcome,
  # Names taken back, in the order they were asked for.
  closes: []string,
  # How many addresses came out of the firewall. Zero with a non-empty 'closes' is a real state:
  # the name was granted and the container never reached it, so nothing was in the set.
  addresses: int,
  # Always false: Porelo changes no project file. With RUN_AND_PROJECT, detail holds what to write.
  persisted: bool,
  # Why it was refused or what went wrong. "" otherwise.
  detail: string
)

method WidenTask(
  # Container name, from Task.name.
  task: string,
  # Host names to grant. A grant covers what is under it, as the resolver does.
  domains: []string,
  # Required. RUN or RUN_AND_PROJECT.
  scope: Scope,
  # Stop before changing anything and report what would happen.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  outcome: WidenOutcome,
  # The names it grants, in the order they were asked for.
  opens: []string,
  # Always false: Porelo changes no project file. With RUN_AND_PROJECT, detail holds what to write.
  persisted: bool,
  # Why it was refused; with RUN_AND_PROJECT, the project file and the egress section a person
  # writes into it. "" when there is nothing to say.
  detail: string
)

# Which of the four questions a ReachRow answers.
type ReachPart (
  # What a task can connect to.
  REACH,
  # What a task has in its hands.
  HOLD,
  # What a task can use up.
  SPEND,
  # What a task can leave behind.
  PRODUCE
)

# What kind of guard a ReachRow is.
type ReachKind (
  # Holds even against an agent that tries to get round it.
  CONTROL,
  # Stops the ordinary mistake and nothing more. Never render it as containment.
  ACCIDENT_CATCHER,
  # A path left open on purpose or by nature, named so nobody counts it as closed.
  NOT_BOUNDED
)

# One thing a task can reach, hold, spend or produce, and what bounds it.
type ReachRow (
  part: ReachPart,
  # What is bounded, as the page "How far a task can get" names it. Markdown.
  subject: string,
  # The bound, in the page's words, with every number read off the code that enforces it. Markdown.
  bound: string,
  kind: ReachKind,
  # What this machine's own setting adds to the bound. Absent where nothing on the machine decides.
  machine: ?string,
  # With a task asked about, its own bound where its run was widened or narrowed, or its clearance
  # mode answers differently. Absent where the task has the machine's bound.
  task: ?string
)

# What a task on this machine can reach, hold, spend and produce, and what bounds each.
#
# The rows are the page "How far a task can get", in its order: its tables' rows, then the paths it
# says are not bounded. A test holds the two together, so an interface showing this shows what an
# operator reading the page is told.
#
# With a task, every row is answered for that task: the names its run was granted and not taken
# back, what its clearance mode does with an undeclared reach, and an authority it was handed.
method Reach(
  # Container name, from Task.name. Default: this machine, for no task in particular. A task that
  # does not exist is NoSuchTask.
  task: ?string
) -> (
  rows: []ReachRow
)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- clearance

# Blocked connections from every running task, as they happen.
#
# Streaming only: answering this once would look like it worked and then deliver nothing ever
# again, so a call without "more" is refused.
#
# One subscription covers every task, including tasks started after the call - an interface
# does not discover and connect to each task's own socket.
# The reply is a Prompt: varlink wants the reply spelt out as a struct, so its fields are repeated here.
method Prompts() -> (
  # Container name, for routing the answer back to the right task.
  task: string,
  # Pass this to Decide unchanged. It is derived from the other fields, but the derivation is
  # the daemon's and a client that rebuilt it would answer a prompt that does not exist.
  key: string,
  # Host or address the task asked for.
  destination: string,
  # tcp or udp.
  protocol: string,
  port: int,
  # When it was blocked, ISO-8601 - and on an event carrying a verdict, when it was decided.
  # The two are the same field because they are the same event twice; keep the question's own
  # value if you want the block time of an answered prompt.
  at: string,
  # Where in the ruleset it was stopped, for an operator who needs to know which rule fired.
  # Empty on an event carrying a verdict: nothing decided it a second time.
  prefix: string,
  # When the watcher gives up, ISO-8601, on an open question. "" when it never will, and "" on
  # an event carrying a verdict - nothing is waiting any more.
  #
  # A moment rather than a length, and computed where the timeout lives: an interface showing
  # "three minutes left" would otherwise be doing arithmetic on a number nobody sent it. It is
  # measured from when the question was asked rather than from "at", because "at" is when the
  # connection was blocked and a reader that has fallen behind would publish a deadline already
  # in the past.
  #
  # It belongs to the question, not to the packet. A dropped connection is retried, so the same
  # destination arrives again while one question is open - "at" moves with each, the deadline does
  # not. Recomputing it per event would hand a countdown a fresh minute every retry and it would
  # never visibly run out.
  deadline: string,
  # A destination is announced as open at most once. Its connection is retried and the watcher
  # sees every retry, but a key it has already answered is not published again - so a client that
  # took a settled question off screen never has it put back with buttons under it.
  #
  # Absent while the question is open. Once it is settled the same destination arrives again
  # carrying "allow", "deny" or "timeout", and that is the only way an interface learns that a
  # prompt ran out: nothing will ask about it again, so a question that simply stops arriving is
  # indistinguishable from one still waiting for its operator.
  verdict: ?string,
  # How many events were dropped since the previous one on this stream, because it was read more
  # slowly than they arrived. Absent when none were.
  #
  # A dropped question is not asked again, so its task stays blocked until its deadline with
  # nothing on screen. A client that sees this should tell its operator that questions may be
  # waiting unseen, rather than go on as if it had seen everything.
  missed: ?int
)

# Answers one prompt.
#
# A task that is not running has no watcher to tell, and that is refused rather than accepted
# and dropped: an answer that goes nowhere leaves the operator believing they unblocked
# something.
method Decide(
  # Container name, from Prompt.task.
  task: string,
  # From Prompt.key, unchanged.
  key: string,
  # From Prompt.destination.
  address: string,
  # Whether to let it through.
  allow: ?bool
) -> (ok: bool)

# The task is not running, so nothing can act on the answer.
error NoClearance(task: string)

# WidenTask was called without saying how far the change goes.
error ScopeRequired()

# One entry in the vault. Never its value: this interface cannot be used to read a secret.
type Credential (
  name: string,
  # What kind of credential it is, or "" when nothing recorded it.
  type: string,
  # Length of the stored value, so an interface can show that something is there.
  characters: int,
  # What goes with the secret and is not secret - a client id, a token URL, scopes - by name.
  # Configuration, shown whole; never a secret.
  settings: [string]string,
  # For an entry a person grants (oauth-device, oauth-code): who granted it and when, or absent while
  # nobody has. The grant itself is never shown.
  grant: ?Authorization
)

# A service a credential is for that is not a model provider: where it is, and where its key goes.
type Destination (
  name: string,
  label: string,
  # Where requests go, https.
  upstream: string,
  # The header the key goes in, and the text before it.
  authHeader: string,
  authPrefix: string,
  # The URL parameter the key goes in instead of the header, or "" for the header.
  authQuery: string,
  # The file that declares it.
  file: string,
  # Whether a package installed it. A packaged one cannot be changed or removed here; writing one of
  # the same name puts the user's own in its place.
  packaged: bool,
  # Whether it is the one in force for its name. A packaged one the user's own takes the place of is
  # listed too, with this false.
  inForce: bool
)

# What the vault holds, without any of it.
method Credentials() -> (
  # Where the vault file is.
  vault: string,
  # Whether that file exists.
  exists: bool,
  credentials: []Credential,
  # Whether the list can be believed. The vault opens only if its passphrase is already in the
  # kernel keyring - a daemon has no terminal to ask at - so an empty list from a locked vault
  # and an empty list from an empty vault are different things an interface must not conflate.
  # False means locked or undecryptable, and only then is there a passphrase to ask for. A vault
  # that does not exist yet is readable and empty: there is nothing to unlock.
  readable: bool,
  # What this machine is CONFIGURED to connect with, which is a different list from the one above.
  # Readable with the vault shut, because it holds no secret - which is what lets an interface say
  # "a credential for this host is configured, unlock the vault" instead of "nothing is here".
  connections: []Connection
)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- connecting out

# One credential this machine may use to reach somewhere, described without its secret.
#
# The metadata is NOT secret: what exists, for what, of what kind, under which name. That is why an
# interface may write these records (CredentialDeclare) while no value ever crosses this socket.
type Connection (
  # What the secret is called: a vault entry, a file path, or an environment variable, by source.
  id: string,
  # SSH_KEY, TOKEN, BASIC or OAUTH. Said, never inferred: https may be a token OR a username and
  # password, so the URL cannot decide it.
  kind: string,
  # The destinations this covers, as a prefix of the normalised URL - "ssh://github.com" or
  # "https://gitlab.example/acme/". git@host:path normalises to ssh://host/path. The LONGEST match
  # wins, so one record covers a forge and another covers one group on it.
  match: string,
  # Username, for BASIC and for a token that needs one. "" when the caller's default is fine.
  user: string,
  # What it may be used for: "git", "registry", ... or "any".
  purpose: string,
  # VAULT, FILE, ENVIRONMENT or AGENT.
  #
  # FILE and ENVIRONMENT point at something this machine did not create and does not protect - a
  # key in ~/.ssh, a token already exported - which is what somebody working on their own computer
  # wants, and is a state to SHOW rather than a fault. AGENT reads nothing at all: the command
  # inherits the account's own ssh-agent.
  source: string,
  # Whether the secret is somewhere this machine encrypts and can shut. False for FILE,
  # ENVIRONMENT and AGENT. Show it; do not refuse it.
  protected: bool,
  # Whether the secret is actually there right now: an entry in the vault, a readable file, a set
  # variable. False with a shut vault only tells you the vault is shut - read 'readable' too.
  present: bool,
  # When an OAUTH token stops working, RFC 3339, or "". A machine that knows this refuses first
  # and says what to run, instead of letting the destination answer 401.
  expires: string
)

# Records a credential this machine may use. NO SECRET CROSSES THIS SOCKET.
#
# What this writes is the readable half - kind, destination, username, where the value lives. The
# value itself is typed on the machine, and 'storeCommand' says exactly what to type. For source
# FILE, ENVIRONMENT or AGENT there is nothing to type: the value is already somewhere, and this
# records where.
method CredentialDeclare(
  # What the secret is called. For VAULT the entry name; for FILE the path; for ENVIRONMENT the
  # variable. For AGENT, one SHA256 fingerprint or public key line; omitted means all agent keys.
  #
  # Omitted for VAULT too, which means "you name it": the name becomes the one this machine
  # already looks in for that destination - git.ssh.<host> or git.token.<host> - so a credential
  # declared without a name and one stored without a declaration are the same entry. A destination
  # that names no host is refused rather than recorded namelessly.
  id: ?string,
  kind: string,
  match: string,
  user: ?string,
  purpose: ?string,
  # VAULT (the default), FILE, ENVIRONMENT or AGENT.
  source: ?string,
  # A file ON THAT MACHINE the value is in. Only with source VAULT, and only a convenience: the
  # answer's storeCommand then reads that file instead of asking for the value to be piped or
  # typed, so "use the key that is already here" is one command a client runs unchanged. Nothing
  # is read here - the command does it, when somebody runs it.
  fromFile: ?string,
  # Says what declaring this would do, and writes NOTHING. Every refusal a real declaration gives:
  # a kind that cannot open that destination, a public key, a value that is not there, a shut
  # vault. For an ssh key it also asks the forge who that key logs in as - see 'identity' - which
  # catches a key belonging to another account or another repository at the moment somebody
  # chooses it, rather than at the first fetch.
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (
  # The record as it was written.
  connection: Connection,
  # What to run ON THAT MACHINE to store the value, as ARGUMENTS rather than a string: a client
  # runs it over ssh and should never have to split a command line it did not write. Empty when
  # there is nothing to store - FILE, ENVIRONMENT and AGENT already have their value somewhere.
  storeCommand: []string,
  # What that command needs on its standard input, in words, or "" when it prompts for the value
  # itself. An ssh KEY is a file and cannot be typed at a prompt, so this says "the private key
  # file" and the client arranges for it; a token is asked for, without echo, and this is "".
  storeStdin: string,
  # Whether this replaced a record that was already there for the same destination and purpose.
  # Declaring twice is not an error - a wizard run again must land in the same place - so this is
  # how an interface says "updated" rather than "added".
  replaced: bool,
  # Whether anything was written. False for a dry run.
  recorded: bool,
  # READY or the reason it would not work, for a dry run. "" for a real declaration, which either
  # succeeds or fails with an error.
  #
  # READY, MISSING_VALUE, NO_VAULT, VAULT_LOCKED, NO_CREDENTIAL, EXPIRED or KEY_REFUSED.
  # NO_VAULT is not MISSING_VALUE: there is nowhere to put anything yet, so 'porelo vault init'
  # comes first and a wizard that sent somebody to 'vault put' would fail at its last step.
  # KEY_REFUSED means the host turned this key away - the record would be perfect and the first
  # fetch would fail.
  outcome: string,
  # What the forge said when asked who this key logs in as, for a dry run of an SSH_KEY - either
  # "Hi fuinorg/utils4j!" or what it refused with - and "" when nothing could be asked. A key that
  # reaches a different account, or a single other repository, or nothing at all, looks perfectly
  # good until the first fetch, and only the machine can ask. When it was refused, 'outcome' is
  # KEY_REFUSED: silence about that would read exactly like "nothing to report".
  identity: string,
  # One sentence for a person, for a dry run. "" otherwise.
  detail: string
)

# Forgets a credential record. The secret is NOT removed: a key in ~/.ssh is the person's, and a
# vault entry is removed with CredentialRemove, or 'porelo vault remove' at the machine.
method CredentialForget(
  # The record's 'match', exactly as Connection gives it.
  match: string
) -> (
  # Whether a record was there to forget.
  forgotten: bool,
  # What still holds a value, so an interface can offer the second step rather than implying the
  # secret is gone. "" when nothing does.
  leftBehind: string
)

# Removes a vault entry and its value - what 'porelo vault remove' does at the machine, from the same
# code - with the client secret a sign-in left beside it.
#
# Only an entry Credentials lists. One whose grant or client registration has to be ended at its
# service first is refused as GRANTED: that is 'porelo vault remove' at the machine, which can ask.
method CredentialRemove(
  # The entry, as Credentials names it.
  name: string
) -> ()

# Replaces the value of a vault entry, keeping its kind and settings - what 'porelo vault put' does at
# the machine, from the same code. The next task reads the new value. A replace never creates an entry.
#
# The value crosses this socket once, inbound: it is never logged, echoed or answered, and no
# refusal carries any part of it.
method CredentialReplace(
  # The entry, as Credentials names it.
  name: string,
  # The new secret. A private key is kept as 'porelo vault put' keeps it, and read back first.
  value: string
) -> (
  # Whether the vault was written; false when the entry already held exactly this.
  changed: bool
)

# A vault entry was not removed or replaced, and nothing changed. Never carries any part of a value.
#
# reason is NO_VAULT, VAULT_LOCKED (the vault is shut: nothing held opens it, and the daemon has no
# terminal to ask at), NO_SUCH_CREDENTIAL, RESERVED (a task's or a grant's own entry, which
# Credentials never lists), GRANTED, BORROWED (a running task holds it until it stops; tasks names
# them), NO_VALUE or VALUE_UNREADABLE (a private key this machine cannot sign with).
error CredentialRefused(reason: string, name: string, tasks: []string)

# One ssh key this account has, described without its value.
type SshKey (
  # Where it is. This is what a FILE credential names.
  path: string,
  # The algorithm, or "" when a passphrase hides it - an encrypted key says nothing about itself.
  type: string,
  # SHA256:... of the public half, or "" when it cannot be worked out.
  fingerprint: string,
  # What the public half calls it, usually an address. Often the only thing that tells two keys
  # apart to the person who made them.
  comment: string,
  # Whether a passphrase protects it.
  encrypted: bool,
  # Whether the PRIVATE half is there. False means only a .pub was found - which is not a
  # credential, and is the mistake anybody makes once.
  privateHalf: bool,
  # Whether this machine can sign with it as it stands.
  usable: bool,
  # DIRECTORY, CONFIGURED (IdentityFile in ~/.ssh/config), or AGENT (public identity from ssh-agent).
  found: string,
  # Why it cannot be used as it stands, with what to do about it, or "".
  obstacle: string,
  # Public half for an AGENT key, empty for files. No private value crosses this socket.
  publicKey: ?string
)

# The ssh keys this account already has.
#
# So that "use the key that is already here" is a choice from a list rather than a path typed from
# memory. NO VALUE is ever read out of here: a key is opened far enough to say what it is and
# whether a passphrase protects it.
#
# Deciding what is a private key means reading files, which is why this is answered by the machine
# rather than by a client listing a home directory over ssh.
method SshKeys() -> (
  keys: []SshKey
)

# Which credential a destination would use, and whether it would work - before anything is tried.
#
# The call a dialog makes before offering 'Approve', without touching the network.
method CredentialCheck(
  # Where something would connect, such as a project repository's URL.
  url: string,
  # What for. Default: "git".
  purpose: ?string
) -> (
  # READY, NO_CREDENTIAL, VAULT_LOCKED, MISSING_VALUE, EXPIRED or NOT_NEEDED.
  #
  # NOT_NEEDED is a local path: nothing authenticates, and offering to store something would waste
  # somebody's afternoon. MISSING_VALUE means a record exists and its value does not - the file is
  # gone, the variable is unset, the vault entry was never stored.
  outcome: string,
  # The record that would be used, or an empty one.
  connection: Connection,
  # What to run on that machine to fix it, as arguments. Empty when nothing would help - a local
  # path, or a value this machine does not keep.
  storeCommand: []string,
  # What that command needs on its standard input, or "". See CredentialDeclare.
  storeStdin: string,
  # One sentence for a person.
  detail: string
)

# Copies a credential an agent already holds on this machine into the vault.
#
# The one credential operation that carries no secret over this socket in either direction: the
# daemon reads a file on its own disk and writes its own vault, and all that crosses is an agent's
# name. So it is unaffected by whether storing a typed secret is ever allowed.
#
# It is the better first offer wherever an agent can log itself in, because nothing has to be
# retyped - and the commonest typo when retyping is storing the guide's placeholder.
method ImportCredential(
  # Which agent to import from. Omit when only one is installed.
  agent: ?string,
  # Where it keeps its credentials. Omit for what the agent declares.
  configDirectory: ?string,
  # The provider whose sign-in to import, stored under that name. Omit for the one the agent
  # answers, which is stored under the provider it names, or else under its default.
  provider: ?string
) -> (
  # IMPORTED, NO_SUCH_AGENT, NO_CONFIG_DIRECTORY, NOTHING_TO_IMPORT, VAULT_LOCKED, WRONG_PROVIDER
  # or FAILED.
  #
  # NOTHING_TO_IMPORT is the ordinary case rather than a fault: the agent is installed and nobody
  # has logged in with it here yet. VAULT_LOCKED is not a missing credential and must not be shown
  # as one - it is "we cannot tell you until you unlock it at the machine". WRONG_PROVIDER stores
  # nothing: this agent cannot drive that provider, or its answer named another or none.
  outcome: string,
  # The vault key it was stored under. "" when nothing was stored.
  name: string,
  # The kind, as the agent recorded it. "" when nothing was stored.
  type: string,
  # How many characters arrived, so somebody can see that it worked without seeing what worked.
  # Never the value. 0 when nothing was stored.
  length: int,
  # The file it was read from, on the daemon's machine. "" when nothing was stored.
  source: string,
  # Why it failed, or a warning about what was stored - a credential that looks like a
  # placeholder, or one a task will refuse. "" when there is nothing to say.
  detail: string
)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- keyslots

# What can open this machine's vault.
#
# The vault's master key is stored only in wrapped form, once per credential allowed to open it.
# A passphrase vault has a recovery slot. A vault created by 'vault init --share-stdin' has only an
# ssh-agent slot and no passphrase recovery. Its supplied 32 bytes wrap the master key directly;
# USER_SCOPED records that another process with access to the person's agent can obtain the share.
# A device slot keeps a 32-byte share in its own platform's keystore. Adding a device adds a slot, removing one deletes a
# slot, and neither re-keys anything nor disturbs another device.
#
# Readable while the vault is locked: a screen has to show which devices can open a vault before
# anything opens it. Nothing here is secret.
method Keyslots() -> (slots: []Keyslot)

type Keyslot (
  # Assigned by the node and kept by the device beside its share. Not derived from the share: the
  # node discards that at enrollment and could derive nothing from it later.
  id: string,
  # What a person called this device. Shown in a list; never an identifier.
  name: string,
  # USER_SCOPED, APPLICATION_SCOPED, FIDO2 or TPM2, as the device declared it. Recorded, not
  # verified - this machine cannot check what another one does with its own keystore.
  storage: string,
  enrolled: string,
  # "" when it has not been used since it was enrolled.
  lastUsed: string,
  # True only for the slot whose share this machine is currently holding, so a list can mark
  # "this device". About the session, not about the file.
  self: bool,
  # True for the passphrase, which is keyslot 0, is recovery only, and is not a device.
  recovery: bool
)

# Lets a device open the vault from now on.
#
# The share arrives once, wraps the master key, and is discarded. It is never logged, never echoed
# back and never written anywhere. What is left here is worth nothing without the device, and what
# the device holds is worth nothing without this machine's vault file.
method EnrollDevice(
  # What to call the device in a list.
  name: string,
  # 32 random bytes, base64. The device generates them and keeps them in its platform's keystore.
  share: string,
  # USER_SCOPED, APPLICATION_SCOPED, FIDO2 or TPM2.
  storage: string
) -> (
  # ENROLLED, ALREADY_ENROLLED, UNKNOWN_STORAGE, BAD_SHARE, VAULT_LOCKED,
  # VAULT_WITHOUT_KEYSLOTS or FAILED.
  outcome: string,
  slot: ?Keyslot,
  detail: string
)

# Takes a device's way in away.
#
# Deleting one wrapped blob and nothing else: no re-keying, no other device disturbed, no
# passphrase rotated.
method RevokeKeyslot(id: string) -> (
  # REVOKED, NO_SUCH_SLOT, LAST_WAY_IN, VAULT_LOCKED, VAULT_WITHOUT_KEYSLOTS or FAILED.
  outcome: string,
  # What can still open the vault afterwards, so a screen can say it without asking again.
  remaining: []Keyslot,
  detail: string
)

# Opens the vault with a share.
#
# A device's share is held in the kernel keyring with a timeout. A host vault's share is held in
# the daemon's locked memory until lock or exit, and refuses minutes. The unwrapped master key
# is not retained, since it would outlive every revocation.
# An SSH share for a vault containing other slot kinds is refused with NotAHostVault.
method UnlockWithShare(
  # When supplied, must resolve to this daemon's vault.
  vault: ?string,
  # The opening share, base64.
  share: string,
  # Device shares only: how long, in minutes. Absent means eight hours; omit for a host share.
  minutes: ?int
) -> (
  # UNLOCKED, SHARE_REJECTED, BAD_SHARE, VAULT_WITHOUT_KEYSLOTS or FAILED.
  # SHARE_REJECTED means no slot takes the share; a malformed file or content authentication failure
  # is FAILED. Rejection never authorizes clearing the vault.
  outcome: string,
  # When the unlock runs out, RFC 3339, or "".
  until: string,
  slot: ?Keyslot,
  detail: string
)

# What a person has to authorize before work can use it. Streaming only.
#
# First what is open now, then what changes: a start refused for want of a grant (state "never"), a grant
# the broker found revoked or expired at the service ("ended"), and "granted" when a grant lands - by
# Authorize, 'porelo vault authorize', or whoever answered. Raised once per credential until it is granted,
# not once per refused start. Answering it is Authorize with 'credential'.
# The reply is a AuthorizationNeeded: varlink wants the reply spelt out as a struct, so its fields are repeated here.
method Authorizations() -> (
  # The vault entry that names the service; pass it to Authorize as 'name'.
  credential: string,
  # The task that needed it, or "".
  task: string,
  # That task's project, or "".
  project: string,
  # never, ended or granted.
  state: string,
  # When it was raised - or, for granted, when the grant was seen - ISO 8601.
  at: string
)

# Grants an authorization once, for this account's later tasks. Streaming only.
#
# The first reply is state "needed", with the link to open, whole. For a device code (kind oauth-device) it
# carries the code to type if the link does not, and the person decides in any browser anywhere. For a
# redirect (kind oauth-code) the code is empty and port is the loopback port on the machine the answer comes
# back to: a client whose browser is elsewhere forwards that port (ssh -L port:127.0.0.1:port) before opening
# the link. The last reply is "granted", "refused", "expired" or "failed" (with a detail). The grant is kept
# in the account's vault, hidden, with who granted it and when; no task holds it. A service that grants a
# token which never expires and nothing to renew it - GitHub's device flow for an OAuth app - is kept as it
# is: "granted", with a detail saying it cannot be revoked from here. One that expires with nothing to renew
# it is "refused", saying why.
method Authorize(
  # The vault entry of kind oauth-device or oauth-code that names the service.
  name: string
) -> (state: string, link: string, code: string, port: int, expiresIn: int, detail: string)

# Every destination declared on this machine, the one in force for each name first.
method Destinations() -> (destinations: []Destination)

# One destination, as it is in force.
method Destination(name: string) -> (destination: Destination)

# Declares a destination in the user's own directory, taking the place of a packaged one of the same name.
#
# With dryRun, nothing is written and the answer says what would be: the destination as it would be read
# back, or the refusal - a name that is not one, an upstream that is not https, no header.
method WriteDestination(
  name: string,
  label: ?string,
  upstream: string,
  # Default: "Authorization".
  authHeader: ?string,
  authPrefix: ?string,
  authQuery: ?string,
  dryRun: ?bool
) -> (destination: Destination, written: bool)

# Removes the user's own declaration of a destination. A packaged one cannot be removed; if one of the same
# name exists, it is in force again afterwards.
method RemoveDestination(name: string) -> (removed: bool, file: string)

# The destination as given is not one Porelo can use; the message says why.
error DestinationRefused(message: string)

# Clears the vault from this machine in one step - 'porelo vault clear', from the same code.
#
# First each transport clears the account a project keeps on a server it names itself, while the vault still
# holds its token; then the vault file, its backup and its lock go, with every device's keyslot in it, and
# the passphrase and shares the kernel keyring caches for it; then each transport's account-wide clear runs.
# What the vault held is named: credentials, grants, the accounts a transport kept, stopped tasks' tokens.
# Its deploy keys are answered in keys, for a person to remove at the forge. Afterwards 'porelo vault init'
# starts as on a new machine.
#
# A running task that holds a token from the vault refuses it, force or not: stop the task first.
method VaultClear(
  # When supplied, must resolve to this daemon's vault.
  vault: ?string,
  # Says what would go, and removes nothing.
  dryRun: ?bool,
  # Clears a locked vault unread, or one whose transport could not clear a project's account.
  force: ?bool
) -> (
  # Each thing, in the order it was handled.
  items: []ClearedItem,
  # The deploy keys this machine had at forges, for a person to remove there by title and fingerprint.
  keys: []MachineDeployKey,
  # Whether it stopped before removing the vault: a running task holding a token, a locked vault, or a
  # transport that could not clear a project's account.
  refused: bool,
  # Why it kept the vault, for a reader deciding on force: tasks (force does not get past it), locked, or
  # transports; "" when it did not.
  kept: string
)

# Internal host helpers use the account's owner-only socket; shares are transient base64 here.
# Paths resolve to the daemon's vault, whose wrapped key must still match the held share.
# Every method requires SO_PEERCRED's uid to match this daemon's, including SSH connections.
# Forget compares the vault location without requiring the encrypted file to exist.
method KeepHostVaultShare(vault: string, share: string) -> ()
method HostVaultShare(vault: string) -> (open: bool)
method ReadHostVaultShare(vault: string) -> (share: string)
method ForgetHostVaultShare(vault: string) -> (wasHeld: bool)

# The offered share opens no slot; the vault has not been cleared or changed.
error OtherVaultKey()

# The vault contains passphrase or other slots; host-key unlock and reset are refused.
error NotAHostVault()

# A push waiting at the gate for a decision.
type PendingPush (
  # Ref name under refs/porelo/incoming/, which is what Review, Approve and Reject take.
  name: string,
  commit: string,
  # First line of the commit message.
  subject: string,
  # How long it has been waiting, as words: "4 minutes".
  waiting: string,
  # When it arrived, ISO-8601.
  at: string,
  # The `git fetch` that brings this push into a person's own clone of the repository, over their
  # ssh, as "git fetch ssh://<user>@<host><mirror> refs/porelo/incoming/<name>:refs/remotes/porelo/<name>".
  # The host is this machine's own name; a client that reached it by another may put that one in.
  # Reading only: the work is the agent's and not reviewed - say to open it in the IDE's safe mode,
  # and never to build or test it outside a container.
  fetch: string,
  # Whether the upstream already has this work: somebody pushed it there by hand, past the gate, as the last
  # measurement of the upstream found. Reject refuses it without 'force'. Absent from a daemon before this field.
  alreadyUpstream: ?bool
)

# One file a pending push changes, as a person should meet it in a review.
#
# Ranked by consequence, not by suspicion: nothing here detects anything. DANGEROUS is a fixed list of
# kinds Porelo ships - a CI definition, a build script, a dependency manifest or lockfile, something that
# runs on checkout or when the repository is opened, a file made executable, a symbolic link - and is
# named however small the change. GENERATED and REFORMATTING are what is almost never the finding; they
# are moved to the end, never hidden, and a dangerous file is never either.
type ReviewFile (
  # Its path in the repository, after the change. A rename is a removal and an addition.
  path: string,
  # A, M, D or T, as git reports it.
  status: string,
  # Lines added and removed; -1 for a binary file.
  added: int,
  removed: int,
  # Counts over the same range, with git's --ignore-space-at-eol; -1 for binary files.
  # Absent from older daemons. This also ignores trailing spaces, not only CRLF changes.
  addedIgnoringLineEndings: ?int,
  removedIgnoringLineEndings: ?int,
  # DANGEROUS, ORDINARY, GENERATED or REFORMATTING - the order to read them in.
  rank: string,
  # Why it has that rank, in words; "" for an ORDINARY file.
  reason: string,
  # The delivery this file came in through: its content at the push is exactly a file Porelo
  # handed into the task, at any path. Absent for the agent's own work, and from older daemons.
  origin: ?DeliveredOrigin
)

# Where a pushed file came from, when Porelo delivered its content into the task.
type DeliveredOrigin (
  # HAND_IN: a file handed into /porelo/files.
  kind: string,
  # The delivered file's name.
  name: string,
  # Who delivered it: an account, or "porelo".
  by: string,
  # When, UTC.
  at: string,
  # The delivered content's sha256, hex.
  sha256: string
)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- the gate

# What is waiting at a project's gate.
#
# The gate methods take a project NAME rather than a task, because a gate belongs to a project and
# one daemon serves every project the operator has.
#
# A NAME and not a path: a client cannot see this machine's filesystem and has no path to invent,
# and which project a call is about must not depend on a directory. The machine resolves it, and
# prefers the file it verified - so a call and a task cannot end up reading two different files.
#
# Each of them also takes 'repository', because a gate belongs to ONE REPOSITORY of that project:
# every repository has its own mirror, its own review branch and its own answer to "what is waiting
# for review". Omitting it means the project's own repository - the one the project file belongs
# to - which is the only default in this interface that is not Porelo picking between equals.
# Project.repositories lists the names.
method Pending(
  # Project NAME, as Projects.name gives it.
  project: string,
  # Upstream to gate against. Default: the project's own.
  upstream: ?string,
  # Which repository of the project. Default: the project's own.
  repository: ?string
) -> (
  # Path to the bare mirror the agent pushes into.
  mirror: string,
  # offline, gatekeeping or online.
  mode: string,
  # Where the mirror was seeded from, or "" when it was not.
  seededFrom: string,
  pending: []PendingPush
)

# What a pending push contains, so it can be judged before it is forwarded.
method Review(
  project: string,
  upstream: ?string,
  # Which repository of the project. Default: the project's own.
  repository: ?string,
  # Ref name, from PendingPush.name.
  name: string,
  # What to diff against. Default: the task's recorded starting branch, or the mirror's HEAD for older tasks.
  against: ?string,
  # Ignore whitespace at line endings in the diff (git --ignore-space-at-eol). Default: false.
  # Counts, file ranking and log are unchanged by this switch.
  ignoreLineEndings: ?bool
) -> (
  # Unified diff, in the order of files: the dangerous first, reformatting and generated files last.
  diff: string,
  # Commit log over the same range.
  log: string,
  # What the push changes, ranked; the diff shows them in this order.
  files: []ReviewFile,
  # What the task that pushed this was asked, to show before the files - never compared with them.
  # "" when it was started without an instruction; absent when the task is gone from this machine.
  asked: ?string
)

# Forwards a pending push upstream. The only call in this interface that sends anything
# anywhere, and it makes the caller name the branch rather than inferring one - except in a project
# with a branch block, where the task's own recorded branch is used, or on the host's own forge,
# where porelo/<task> is the default. On that forge approval opens or reuses a pull request with
# the task's token, targeting the repository's default branch. If the API refuses, Failed says
# the push happened and why the forge refused; the approval stays pending for a retry.
method Approve(
  project: string,
  upstream: ?string,
  # Which repository of the project. Default: the project's own.
  repository: ?string,
  name: string,
  # Branch to push to. On the host's forge, omitted means porelo/<task> without a branch block.
  # Elsewhere required in a project without a branch block (BranchRequired otherwise). In a
  # project with one, empty means the task's own branch (Task.forgeBranch), and a named one must match its
  # branch.pattern, or the answer is BranchRefused.
  branch: ?string,
  # Merge the work onto the branch as a commit signed with the signing key the daemon's user has configured
  # in git (user.signingkey; gpg.format ssh for an ssh key), and push that, rather than pushing the work as
  # it is. For what the project's configuration is made of - a machine's enrolment - which every machine
  # accepts only signed with the project's key. The machine never holds the key; git signs. Refused as
  # Failed when the merge cannot be made or comes out unsigned. From the daemon an ssh-agent is reached only
  # if its socket is in the daemon's environment; 'porelo gate approve --signed' in a terminal always is.
  signed: ?bool,
  # The commit the person reviewed, as Pending answers it. Refused as MovedSinceReview when the push has moved since,
  # so what goes is what was read; the task's gate stays up during a review. Empty: whatever it holds now.
  # Refused as BranchExists when 'branch' holds work the commit did not grow from, unless its head
  # is, on the host's forge, the last commit this gate forwarded for this task; that head is then
  # replaced with a lease.
  commit: ?string
) -> (
  forwarded: string,
  branch: string,
  # Address of the new or reused pull request on the host's forge; absent elsewhere.
  pullRequest: ?string
)

# Pending work as a git bundle, for a person who merges it on their own computer - signed with their own key -
# and pushes it: what is on the pending ref and not on 'branch' in the mirror (the whole history when the mirror
# has no such branch). Fetch it with 'git fetch <file> refs/porelo/incoming/<name>'. Refused as BundleTooLarge
# above the limit, rather than sent: a reply is one message.
method PendingBundle(
  project: string,
  upstream: ?string,
  # Which repository of the project. Default: the project's own.
  repository: ?string,
  name: string,
  # The branch it is to be merged onto. Default: the recorded starting branch (main for older tasks); with a branch block, the task's own
  # branch, and a named one must match its branch.pattern (BranchRefused otherwise).
  branch: ?string
) -> (
  # The bundle, base64.
  bundle: string,
  bytes: int
)

# Clears pending work that was merged elsewhere and pushed, once the upstream's 'branch' holds its commit -
# fetched with this machine's own credential. Not rejected, which it was not: it went, just not through here.
# landed false changes nothing, and 'detail' says why: not on the branch yet, the upstream not reachable, no
# upstream configured.
method Landed(
  project: string,
  upstream: ?string,
  # Which repository of the project. Default: the project's own.
  repository: ?string,
  name: string,
  # The upstream branch it was merged onto. Required.
  branch: string
) -> (landed: bool, commit: string, detail: string)

# The bundle is larger than a reply may be.
error BundleTooLarge(bytes: int, limit: int)

# Drops a pending push. The work stays in the mirror; only the request is gone.
#
# The task the work came from is told in its inbox, with the person's words when 'reason' gives them - an
# agent whose work silently vanished hands it over again. 'told' is false when no task of that name has a
# mailbox here any more.
# 'force' discards a push the upstream already has (PendingPush.alreadyUpstream); without it such a push is
# refused with AlreadyUpstream and nothing is discarded.
method Reject(project: string, upstream: ?string, repository: ?string, name: string, reason: ?string, force: ?bool)
    -> (rejected: string, told: bool)

# Reject was asked to discard a push the upstream already has, and was not given 'force': nothing was discarded.
error AlreadyUpstream(name: string)

# Approve was given the commit the person reviewed, and the push has moved since: nothing was
# forwarded. Review it again (now is what it holds) and approve that.
error MovedSinceReview(name: string, reviewed: string, now: string)

# Approve was called without a branch to push to, in a project with no branch block - or in one with
# a block, for a task whose branch was not recorded.
error BranchRequired(name: string)

# Approve or PendingBundle named a branch the project's branch.pattern does not accept, so the forge's
# rules would not cover it: nothing was pushed. Name one the pattern accepts, or none for the task's own.
error BranchRefused(branch: string, pattern: string)

# Approve's branch holds work the reviewed commit did not grow from - earlier work of a task whose name came back:
# nothing was pushed. 'at' is the commit the branch holds. Approve onto another branch, such as the next free
# '<branch>-2'; never a force over it, since it can be somebody's reviewed work.
error BranchExists(branch: string, at: string)

# Tries one SSH identity without saving a credential or publishing work. Uses the existing mirror;
# it never initializes or fetches one. source is AGENT (fingerprint or public key id) or FILE (path).
method CredentialTryPush(
  project: string,
  repository: ?string,
  refspec: string,
  source: string,
  id: string
) -> (
  accepted: bool,
  fingerprint: string,
  message: string
)

# Forwarding could not use the chosen SSH identity. No raw remote output is returned.
error PushRefused(
  # NO_CREDENTIAL, KEY_UNAVAILABLE, or PUSH_REFUSED.
  outcome: string,
  address: string,
  source: string,
  id: string,
  fingerprint: string,
  # DEPLOY_KEY when the forge said so, otherwise UNKNOWN.
  forge: string,
  # A complete command for a person to start in a terminal, as arguments.
  command: []string,
  message: string
)

# An agent binary installed on this machine.
type Agent (
  # Name the binary calls itself by, which is what Start takes.
  name: string,
  # Human-readable name.
  label: string,
  # The command it runs inside the container.
  binary: string,
  # The build this agent pins, from its own manifest, or "" when it pins none. NOT what the
  # binary answers if asked its version: nothing here executes it to find out, so two machines
  # showing the same value are running the same declared build.
  version: string,
  # Where the binary was found.
  from: string,
  # What the agent finds of itself on this machine, asked each time Agents is called, and presence
  # only: SIGNED_IN, NOT_SIGNED_IN or CANNOT_SAY, which an agent built before it can be asked
  # answers and is never not signed in.
  found: string,
  # The provider it was found signed in to, or "".
  foundProvider: string,
  # The paths found, its command on this account's PATH included.
  foundPaths: []string,
  # Hosts this agent needs, added to a task's egress on top of the project's own.
  # How this agent logs itself in, as arguments to its own binary, and where that is described.
  #
  # Declared by the agent, never guessed here: some agents open a browser and some print a link
  # and a code, and only the second kind works on a machine somebody reaches over ssh. A client
  # runs these in a TERMINAL on that machine - the login writes into the agent's own config
  # directory, and 'porelo vault import <agent>' then takes what it wrote into the vault.
  #
  # canLogIn is false for most agents: one whose credential is an API key has nothing to log in
  # to, and the manifest saying nothing is how it says so.
  canLogIn: bool,
  # What to run ON THAT MACHINE, in a terminal, to log this agent in. Empty when it cannot.
  #
  # Answered rather than composed by a client: a client that spells out 'porelo vault login
  # <agent>' is right until the day that verb moves, and then every client is wrong at once.
  loginCommand: []string,
  # What that command will run inside its throwaway container - the agent's own binary and
  # whatever it declared. Shown so a person can see what they are about to start; running it is
  # loginCommand's business, not a client's.
  loginArguments: []string,
  # Where that login is described, or "". Worth reading BEFORE running one on a remote machine.
  loginDocumentation: string,
  # How its sign-in works: "paste-code", "device-code" or "loopback". Absent when the definition
  # names none. A loopback sign-in is refused over SSH, before its container starts.
  loginFlow: ?string,
  allowedDomains: []string,
  # Hosts this agent declares and is deliberately NOT given. Not the same as absent from
  # allowedDomains: these were asked for and refused on purpose, which is worth showing beside
  # what was granted rather than leaving as a silence.
  refusedDomains: []string,
  # What this agent fetches when its image is built, each with the digest that pins it. Empty is
  # a real state and not a gap: an agent that writes its tool into the image fetches nothing, so
  # it has a pinned version and no digest at all.
  artifacts: []InstallArtifact,
  # The identity this agent's commits carry, from its own manifest. This is what tells a person
  # looking at a commit whether an agent or a person wrote it, and it is what the pre-push guard
  # on an operator's own checkout matches on.
  commitsAs: GitIdentity
)

# How an agent signs its work.
type GitIdentity (
  # Author name on its commits.
  name: string,
  # Author address on its commits.
  email: string
)

# One file an agent fetches while its image is built.
type InstallArtifact (
  # Where it is fetched from.
  url: string,
  # Expected SHA-256, or "" when there is none - in which case 'unverified' says whether that is
  # deliberate.
  sha256: string,
  # Absolute path it is installed at.
  target: string,
  # Whether it is knowingly unverifiable. An artifact with no digest and no reason is a mistake;
  # one with a reason is a decision somebody made and can be shown as such.
  unverified: bool,
  # Why it cannot be verified. "" unless 'unverified'.
  reason: string
)

# An installed agent binary that never runs: either an earlier location holds one of the same
# file name, or another binary claimed the same declared name first.
type ShadowedAgent (
  # The binary that is installed and not in use.
  path: string,
  # The one that runs instead. Locations are searched most specific first - the operator's own
  # directory, then the packaged one - so this is a rule and not an accident of scan order.
  usedInstead: string
)

# Agent binaries installed on this machine.
method Agents() -> (
  # One entry per agent NAME, never two: they are held by name, so a name appears once whatever
  # is installed. A second binary claiming a name already taken does not appear here at all - it
  # lands in 'failures'.
  agents: []Agent,
  # Binaries that are installed and could not be asked what they are, by file name. An agent
  # that fails to describe itself is unusable, and leaving it out would read as not installed.
  # A name collision is NOT here: nothing is broken in that case, one copy runs.
  failures: [string]string,
  # Binaries that are installed and never run. Two causes, one consequence: an earlier location
  # holds the same file name, or another binary claimed the same declared name first. Neither has
  # an Agent entry to carry a flag, which is why this is a list rather than a field on one.
  shadowed: []ShadowedAgent
)

# Which model providers this machine has, and which of them work could start against.
#
# The read half of authentication, and it carries no secret: names, kinds and whether something is
# stored, never a value.
#
# Cheap in the ordinary case. It consults the installed agents only when the vault holds a name
# that is not a provider's - the one situation where the key below can be an agent's name - so a
# vault written by a current Porelo costs nothing extra.
method Providers() -> (
  providers: []Provider,
  # Whether the vault could be read at all. False means locked or undecryptable, and then
  # 'authenticated' below is unknown rather than false - the same distinction Credentials draws,
  # for the same reason: "we cannot tell you until you unlock it" and "there is none" send a
  # person to different places.
  readable: bool
)

type Provider (
  # Short name, as an agent names it and as 'vault put' takes it.
  name: string,
  # Human-readable label.
  label: string,
  # Where requests are forwarded to.
  upstream: string,
  # Wire formats it serves, the provider's own first.
  dialects: []string,
  # Whether the vault holds a credential for it. MEANINGLESS unless 'readable' - render it only
  # beside that.
  authenticated: bool,
  # The kind stored: "api-key", "oauth", or "" when none is stored or none was recorded.
  credentialType: string,
  # The name a credential for this provider is stored under. Usually the provider's own name -
  # but a vault written before credentials were keyed by provider answers under the AGENT's name,
  # and that key stays in use so upgrading does not stop anyone authenticating.
  #
  # Computed here because a client cannot: intersecting "providers" with "stored names" reports a
  # missing credential for precisely the vault that has one, since the fallback key is invisible
  # from outside.
  credentialName: string,
  # The exact line to run at the machine to store one, ready to render verbatim. Always with the
  # provider's name, even when 'credentialName' is still an agent's: that is where it belongs, and
  # a task finds it there first. A subscription
  # token rather than an API key needs '--type oauth' on the end - they go in different headers,
  # and sending one as the other fails as an authentication error that looks like a wrong key.
  storeCommand: string
)

# What this machine could install, from its own package source.
#
# Nobody maintains a list: every agent package declares 'Provides: porelo-agent' and every transport
# 'Provides: porelo-transport', so the repository is the catalogue and a package published today is
# offered today. The names here are always real packages, never the virtual one - a virtual package
# with several providers cannot be installed by its own name.
#
# The message filter is deliberately absent. It is not one of a kind to choose from: it is the one
# thing without which nothing leaves, and it is always installed.
#
# As fresh as the machine's last index refresh, which needs root and is not done here.
method Installable() -> (packages: []Installable)

type Installable (
  # The real package name, which is what may be installed.
  name: string,
  # 'agent', 'transport' or 'homeserver'. What a person chooses between - the virtual package name that
  # this was read from ('porelo-agent', 'porelo-transport', 'porelo-homeserver') is the mechanism and is not
  # returned. A homeserver is what a transport's conversation runs on when the project names none of its
  # own; offer it beside the transport that needs it.
  kind: string,
  # One line, from the package itself.
  description: string,
  # Whether it is on this machine now.
  installed: bool,
  # What is installed, or what the repository offers. "" when neither is known.
  version: string
)

# The ecosystem adapters installed on this machine and, for a checkout, what each would place there at a first start,
# found as FirstStart finds them: the account's own directory first, then the packaged one, each asked Detect with the
# checkout's directory and the person's home. Nothing is looked up (no Resolve, so nothing reaches the network), and
# nothing is written, in the checkout or on the machine. A checkout of 'default' or of a placed project is asked alike.
# A checkout in no git repository, or not named by its absolute path, is FirstStartRefused with NOT_A_CHECKOUT.
method Ecosystems(
  # The checkout to ask about, by its absolute path. Without it only the adapters are listed.
  checkout: ?string
) -> (
  # Every installed adapter by name, one refused included, which is never asked about a checkout.
  ecosystems: []Ecosystem,
  # With a checkout: one entry per adapter that is not refused, in the order a first start asks them. Absent without.
  placing: ?[]EcosystemPlacing
)

# An installed ecosystem adapter, as its executable describes itself.
type Ecosystem (
  # Its executable's file name, which is the ecosystem it serves.
  name: string,
  # Where it is installed.
  executable: string,
  # Its own release, as it says it. Absent when it is refused or says none.
  version: ?string,
  # The version of org.fuin.porelo.Ecosystem1 it speaks. Absent when it is refused.
  protocolVersion: ?int,
  # Why it cannot be used, in words. Absent when it can.
  refusal: ?string
)

# What one adapter answers for a checkout. Every text an adapter wrote is made printable.
type EcosystemPlacing (
  # The adapter's name, as in Ecosystem.
  ecosystem: string,
  # DETECTED (it recognized something), NOT_RECOGNIZED (nothing of its tool is there), UNREADABLE (a file it needs
  # cannot be read; note says which) or FAILED (it did not answer, or answered no detection).
  outcome: string,
  # What it recognized, as it goes into a first start's draft. Empty unless DETECTED.
  findings: []EcosystemFinding,
  # What it read and can place in no part, with its words. Empty unless DETECTED.
  unplaced: []EcosystemUnplaced,
  # The settings files a first start places from it in a task. Empty today: a first start asks no adapter's Configure.
  files: []EcosystemFile,
  # What a first start says of an UNREADABLE or FAILED adapter, or "".
  note: string
)

# One value an adapter recognized, as org.fuin.porelo.Ecosystem1's Finding has it.
type EcosystemFinding (
  # TOOLS or RULES.
  part: string,
  key: string,
  value: string,
  # Where it is set; "" with line 0 when it comes from no file.
  file: string,
  line: int,
  # YOURS, CHECKOUT or MACHINE: who wrote it.
  origin: string,
  # Whether it is set where it cannot be read without running something.
  unknown: bool,
  note: string
)

# Something an adapter read and can place in no part.
type EcosystemUnplaced (file: string, line: int, note: string)

# A settings file an adapter would have placed in a task: its path, relative to the task user's home, and whether
# only that user may read it. Never what it holds, which may be a credential.
type EcosystemFile (path: string, ownerOnly: bool)

# The installed adapters could not be listed: detail names the directory.
error EcosystemsNotListed(detail: string)

# Someone who has joined a project's conversation.
type MessageMember (
  # The name join was given.
  person: string,
  # The account the transport made for them.
  user: string
)

# This machine's message key, for an interface that writes its machine-signers line into a project itself.
# It signs no message any more: a task signs with its own certified key, and nothing reads machine-signers.
method MessageKey() -> (principal: string, key: string, line: string, fingerprint: string)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- messages

# The peers this task may address, and what a person decided about each: the project file's, and the
# project's other running tasks when it has a conversation (address '<transport>:', trust vouched).
#
# Two things at once because they are read together: the project says who may be addressed, and
# the mailbox says whether anything currently goes there. An interface that showed only the first
# would show a peer as available while every message to it waits.
method Peers(
  # Container name of the task.
  task: string,
  # The project's name, as the other project methods take it. Its verified project.yml is where the peers are declared.
  project: string
) -> (peers: []TalkPeer)

type TalkPeer (
  # What a message addresses.
  name: string,
  # '<transport>:<rest>'.
  address: string,
  # 'vouched' or 'external'. It decides whether what arrives is checked again here.
  trust: string,
  # How many messages a day this task may exchange with it, each way.
  perDay: int,
  # How many went out to it in the last day, as the budget counts them: a rolling day, so
  # nothing is released by midnight arriving. The next one out is refused at perDay.
  sentToday: int,
  # How many from it reached the agent in the last day, counted the same way.
  receivedToday: int,
  # 'prompt', 'allow', 'deny' or 'off' - clearance's four, with the same meanings. Set once in the
  # project's project.yml: 'mail.rules' for the project's own tasks, its conversation and everyone else,
  # or the peer's own 'mode'.
  mode: string,
  # Whether a person holds everything for this peer, whatever the mode says. For the project: every
  # task of it, including tasks started later, sees the same.
  held: bool
)

# Every mailbox on this machine, as it moves. Streaming only, as Prompts is.
#
# The events come from each task's record, which is written as things happen. There is no second
# copy of the conversation to disagree with it, and an entry never carries what a message said -
# a stream that quoted refused messages would be a second copy of exactly what was refused.
#
# Live only: it says what happens from the moment a client listens, and replays nothing. What is
# already waiting for a person when a client starts is Held.
# The reply is a TalkEvent: varlink wants the reply spelt out as a struct, so its fields are repeated here.
method Talk() -> (
  # Container name of the task whose mailbox this is about.
  task: string,
  # When it happened, RFC 3339.
  at: string,
  # taken, queued, sent, deferred, held, delivered, duplicate, refused (by a person), overridden
  # (delivered by a person although the filter refused it) or read (what the task sent was read where it
  # went, as a transport that confirms reading says; detail gives when).
  event: string,
  # The message's file name.
  message: string,
  # Its message id, or "" when none could be read.
  id: string,
  # The peer it was going to or came from, or "".
  peer: string,
  # Why, for the events that have a why. "" otherwise.
  detail: string
)

# Every message a person may be asked to decide about, as it stands now - for an interface that
# starts after a message was held, which the live stream alone would show as "nothing needs you".
#
# Found exactly as ReadHeld and Release find a message, so the list, the reading and the decision
# cannot disagree. Without what the messages say: reading one is a person's deliberate act, through
# ReadHeld. held and refused-by-filter wait for a decision; refused-by-person and unchecked are
# listed because they are still there, and neither can be sent.
method Held(
  # Container name of one task, or absent for every task on this machine.
  task: ?string
) -> (messages: []HeldMessage)

type HeldMessage (
  # Container name of the task whose mailbox holds it.
  task: string,
  # held, refused-by-filter, refused-by-person or unchecked.
  standing: string,
  # The file, as ReadHeld and Release take it.
  message: string,
  # The message id, or "" when none could be read.
  id: string,
  # ROLE_USER when a person wrote it, ROLE_AGENT when a task did, or "".
  role: string,
  # The peer it was going to or came from, or "".
  peer: string,
  # question, answer, status, review-request or handover, or "".
  kind: string,
  # When this host first recorded it, RFC 3339, or "".
  at: string,
  # Why it is held, or, for a refused one, the filter's own answer.
  reason: string,
  # "out" when this task was sending it, "in" when it was reaching this task - an external peer's
  # message is held on its way in, before any agent sees it. "" for a message held before the
  # record kept directions.
  direction: string
)

# Writes a person's own message into a conversation.
#
# It lands in the outbox and goes out through the filter like any other message: a person is not a
# reason to skip the check. What differs is the role, which says a person wrote it.
#
# NoSuchPeer when the project has no conversation and no peer of that name - nothing would carry it.
method Say(
  # Container name of the task.
  task: string,
  # Who it is addressed to.
  peer: string,
  # question, answer, status, review-request or handover. Empty means question.
  kind: string,
  # What it says.
  text: string,
  # The conversation it belongs to, or empty for a new one.
  context: ?string
) -> (
  # The file written into the outbox.
  message: string,
  # WRITTEN. Not 'sent': the next pass decides that.
  outcome: string
)

# Puts a person's own words straight into a task's inbox, where its agent reads - to the task itself, where
# Say speaks for a person to a peer. It carries role ROLE_USER and metadata from "person"; it never leaves the
# machine, so it is neither signed nor filtered.
method Tell(
  # Container name of the task.
  task: string,
  # What the person says.
  text: string
) -> (
  # The file written into the task's inbox.
  message: string
)

# Reads one message a person is asked to decide about, in full, so they decide about what it says
# rather than releasing it unread: a held one, one the filter refused, or one it could not check.
#
# Looked up exactly as Release looks it up. A refused message holds what was refused, secrets
# included, and is shown to the person asking - at the operator's word, so a person can still decide
# to deliver it. It is never in the stream or the record, which stay without content.
method ReadHeld(
  # Container name of the task.
  task: string,
  # Message id or file name, as Talk, Release and 'porelo talk held' take it.
  id: string
) -> (
  # FOUND, NO_SUCH_MESSAGE or AMBIGUOUS.
  outcome: string,
  # held, refused-by-filter, refused-by-person or unchecked, or "". What Release can do with it follows
  # from this: held and refused-by-filter can be sent, the other two never.
  standing: string,
  # The file, or "".
  message: string,
  # The message id, or "".
  id: string,
  # ROLE_USER when a person wrote it, ROLE_AGENT when a task did, or "".
  role: string,
  # The peer it was going to or came from, or "".
  peer: string,
  # question, answer, status, review-request or handover, or "".
  kind: string,
  # When this host first recorded it, RFC 3339, or "".
  at: string,
  # Its parts, in order, exactly as written: render them as text and never interpret them - no
  # markup, no link resolved, nothing fetched. A part that is not text is named by its media type.
  text: []string,
  # Why it is held, or, for a refused one, the filter's own answer - which rules it broke.
  reason: string,
  # "out" when this task was sending it, "in" when it was reaching this task - an external peer's
  # message is held on its way in, before any agent sees it. "" for a message held before the
  # record kept directions.
  direction: string
)

# Releases one held message, or refuses it for good - or, for one the filter refused, delivers it
# after all, or keeps it refused as a person's decision.
#
# Released or refused, never edited: what the record shows a task said has to be what the task
# said. A refused message delivered after all goes straight to its transport, never through the
# filter's accepted directory, and the record says it went despite the filter. An external peer's
# own filter checks it again. What a person refused for good, and what the filter could not check,
# is never sent.
method Release(
  # Container name of the task.
  task: string,
  # Message id or file name, as Talk and 'porelo talk held' show it.
  id: string,
  # Whether to refuse it for good. The sender is told.
  refuse: ?bool,
  # With refuse: why, in the person's words. The sender is told them with the refusal.
  reason: ?string
) -> (
  # RELEASED, REFUSED, DELIVERED_DESPITE_FILTER, NOT_DELIVERABLE, NO_SUCH_MESSAGE or AMBIGUOUS.
  outcome: string,
  # The file it acted on, or "".
  message: string,
  # The message id, or "".
  id: string,
  # Why, for NOT_DELIVERABLE. "" otherwise.
  detail: string
)

# Holds a peer or releases it, for the whole project: every task of it, including tasks started later.
#
# A mode is not set here: the project's project.yml sets it once, for every task, and a 'mode' given
# here fails with a message that says where.
method Moderate(
  # Ignored: a decision is the project's, not one task's. Accepted so an older client still works.
  task: ?string,
  # The project's name, as the other project methods take it. Its verified project.yml decides whether unread work may leave.
  project: string,
  # The peer to decide about.
  name: string,
  # Whether to hold everything for it, or absent to leave that as it is.
  held: ?bool,
  # Refused when given: a mode is set in project.yml. Accepted so an older client is told why.
  mode: ?string
) -> (name: string, mode: string, held: bool)

# Sets a project's conversation up on this machine, or confirms it, without starting a task: what binding a
# machine to a project runs, so it learns the name this machine goes by there and whether it is let in yet.
# Run again after a person let it in, and 'admitted' turns true. Refused with ConversationRefused, carrying
# the transport's words and code.
method SetUpMessages(project: string) -> (messages: ProjectMessages)

# Lets a person into a project's conversation with an account made for them, or gives it a new password
# with reset. Answered once: the login is in this reply and nowhere else, never kept.
#
# 'login' is the transport's, passed through as it gave it. For Matrix: homeserver, user, room, password,
# and loopback ("true" when the homeserver is this machine's loopback) with port - a client elsewhere then
# forwards that port (ssh -L port:127.0.0.1:port) before its Matrix client reaches the homeserver. 'shown'
# is what a terminal prints.
method JoinMessages(
  project: string,
  # Whom the account is for, as MessageMembers lists them.
  person: string,
  # Give the person's existing account a new password; without it, a second join is MemberExists.
  reset: ?bool
) -> (
  transport: string,
  login: [string]string,
  shown: string
)

# Lets another machine into a project's conversation on this machine's server. Answered once: the
# admission and its secrets are in this reply and nowhere else, never kept here. Refused for an offline
# project, whose messages never leave this machine, and when the transport does not offer it.
method AdmitMachine(
  project: string,
  # The machine let in, by the name its transport goes by.
  machine: string
) -> (
  # What the other machine needs to reach this server, for its project's transport settings: for
  # Matrix homeserver, room and ca_certificate.
  admission: [string]string,
  # What it keeps as its secrets, by the names its transport lists. Never show or log it.
  secrets: [string]string
)

# Takes another machine off this machine's conversation server, asked through one project: out of
# every project's conversation there, and its registration refused. Refused for an offline project,
# which admits nobody.
method DismissMachine(
  project: string,
  machine: string
) -> (
  # What the transport removed; empty when nothing was left.
  removed: []string
)

# Who has joined a project's conversation, by the name join was given.
method MessageMembers(project: string) -> (members: []MessageMember)

# Removes a joined person; user is empty when nothing remained, why explains a kept account.
method RemoveMessageMember(project: string, person: string) -> (user: string, kept: bool, why: string)

# The person has joined already; reset gives them a new password.
error MemberExists(person: string, user: string)

# The person has not joined the project's conversation.
error NoSuchMember(person: string)

# The transport refused; message is its own words, code its exit code (75 temporary, 77 refused, 78
# configuration missing).
error ConversationRefused(message: string, code: int)

# The name is not a task Porelo created.
#
# An error rather than an empty-looking answer, deliberately. WorkHeld answers "readable: false"
# for a task nobody could look inside, and a name that was never a task is not that - conflating
# them would let a client's wrong argument arrive as a legitimate answer, which is the failure
# the Backups documentation gap produced.

# A person's message to a name nothing would carry it to: the project has no conversation and no
# peer of that name. detail says what to change.
error NoSuchPeer(peer: string, detail: string)

# Whether this machine can actually run a task.
#
# Every dependency below fails far from its cause - without nft a container comes up with no
# ruleset, without nsenter a clearance decision cannot reach a running task - so today a person
# learns about them by starting work and watching it behave strangely. This is the question they
# cannot otherwise ask: is this machine able to do the thing I am about to ask of it.
#
# It runs external programs to find out, so it takes a moment and is not something to poll. It is
# still a read: nothing here changes anything.
method Doctor() -> (
  probes: []Probe,
  # False when anything is MISSING. Not a summary a client should re-derive: this is the same rule
  # the CLI exits non-zero on, so the two cannot come to different conclusions about one machine.
  #
  # DEGRADED and UNKNOWN leave this true. The machine runs tasks, and the probes say how well.
  ready: bool
)

# One thing outside Porelo that a task depends on.
type Probe (
  # What is being reported, as an operator would name it.
  name: string,
  # OK, DEGRADED, MISSING or UNKNOWN. UNKNOWN is its own state rather than the good case: a probe
  # that guesses well is indistinguishable from one that works.
  state: string,
  # What was found, in one line.
  detail: string,
  # The single next action. "" only when the state is OK - a probe that fails and names nothing to
  # do about it cannot be constructed on this side, so an interface may render this without
  # checking whether it is there.
  action: string
)

method Node() -> (
  # Stable across restarts, and unique to this node. Random, and derived from nothing: it says
  # only whether two connections lead to the same place.
  #
  # A node is an OS user with a daemon, not a machine, so two accounts on one host answer
  # differently here - which is the case a hostname gets wrong.
  id: string
)

# The daemon's own process, as the kernel shows it to that process.
#
# The daemon holds the vault's reads and is therefore not dumpable: its entries under /proc/<pid>
# belong to root, and its own user can no longer read where it runs from. This is how a client
# learns which binary answers on the socket, for instance after an update.
method Process() -> (
  # Its process id; under systemd, the unit's MainPID.
  pid: int,
  # Where /proc/self/exe points. Ends in " (deleted)" once the file it started from was replaced.
  executable: string
)

# What porelo tighten changes, or would: everything Porelo left under the data and state
# directories and ~/porelo/reviews that anyone but this account can read, write or enter. What a
# task reads through a mount, and what a build copies into an image, is open on purpose and never
# listed. A person decides this; nothing calls it for them.
#
# A dry run changes nothing and lists the same changes the run makes, except one the file system
# refuses only when asked, which the run then lists in left. Refused with Failed when a directory
# cannot be looked through.
method Tighten(dryRun: bool) -> (
  # How many paths were found loose.
  found: int,
  # Each path tightened, or to be in a dry run, parents first.
  changes: []TightenedPath,
  # Each path found loose and not changed, or that would not be. Never left out in silence.
  left: []LeftLoose
)

# One path and its mode before and after, as rw-r--r--.
type TightenedPath (
  path: string,
  from: string,
  to: string
)

# One path tighten did not change.
type LeftLoose (
  path: string,
  # LINK      it is a link by now, and changing it would follow the link
  # REFUSED   another uid owns it, or the file system refused
  # GONE      it went away after it was found, which leaves nothing to read
  reason: string
)

# The account's event log: every security-relevant event of this node - an approval or rejection,
# a clearance at run time, a hand-in, a credential a start gave, a project file applied, every task
# stopped at once - one line each, hash-chained, and each line's hash anchored in the user's systemd
# journal, which the account can append to but not rewrite. A read: nothing here changes anything.
method Events(
  # Only events at or after this instant, as 2026-10-28T03:00:00Z. Omitted, all.
  since: ?string
) -> (events: []Event)

# One event, as the log holds it.
type Event (
  # When it was recorded, ISO-8601.
  at: string,
  # approved, rejected, clearance, hand-in, credential, project-applied or stopped-all; unreadable for
  # a line that cannot be read, its other fields "".
  kind: string,
  # The project, or "".
  project: string,
  # The task's container, or "".
  task: string,
  # What happened, in words and values; never a secret.
  detail: string,
  # This line's hash, and the one before it ("" for the first).
  hash: string,
  previous: string
)

# Checks the log's chain, and every line against the hashes the user's journal holds. 'holds' is
# true only when the chain is whole, every line is anchored but those ahead of the first anchored one
# and older than the oldest hash the journal still holds of this log, and the journal holds no hash
# the log has lost. A log with lines and no hash in the journal does not hold.
method VerifyEvents() -> (
  holds: bool,
  lines: int,
  # The number of the first line whose chain breaks, or 0.
  firstBroken: int,
  # Lines whose hash the journal does not hold, other than those counted in beforeAnchor.
  notAnchored: []int,
  # Hashes the journal holds that no line has: a line changed or removed afterwards.
  missing: []string,
  # Whether the journal could be read at all; false means nothing was checked against it.
  anchorRead: bool,
  # Lines ahead of the first anchored one and older than the oldest hash the journal still holds of
  # this log, and not held: rotated out, vouched for by the chain alone.
  beforeAnchor: int
)
